# Arthur's circle (Nov 2026): a page for every person tied to Arthur in Welsh tradition.
# Ifan asked (12 November 2026) for "a page on as many people as possible related to Arthur".
# House rules (EDITORIAL.md): Arthur is a king, never "myth" or "legend". Welsh tradition holds that he was
# Athrwys ap Meurig, king of Gwent and Glywysing; the people of his circle are told the same way, in the
# words of the tales, poems, Triads and Lives ("the tale tells", "the Triads name"). Nothing negative about them.
# Kings are kings. Same entry format as people2a.py; people2.py gathers this list with the others.
# GROUPS sets out the "Arthur's circle" block shown on Arthur's page and on every page in the circle (build.py).
# Dates: "Age of Arthur" is used for people known only from the tales; data.py sorts them just after Arthur.
from people2a import P

PEOPLE = [
 # ---------- Arthur's line: the house of Gwent and Glywysing ----------
 P("tewdrig", "saints", "fl. c. 600", "Tewdrig ap Llywarch", "Tewdrig ap Llywarch", "King, saint and martyr, Arthur’s grandfather", "Brenin, sant a merthyr, taid Arthur", "Tewdrig", "rulers",
   "King of Gwent and Glywysing who gave up his crown to live as a hermit, then rode out once more to drive the Saxons back at Tintern. In Welsh tradition he was the grandfather of Arthur.",
   "Brenin Gwent a Glywysing a roddodd ei goron heibio i fyw’n feudwy, ac a farchogodd allan unwaith eto i yrru’r Saeson yn ôl yn Nhyndyrn. Yn nhraddodiad Cymru, ef oedd taid Arthur.",
   ["Tewdrig ap Llywarch ruled Gwent and Glywysing, the kingdoms of the south-east between the Tawe and the Wye. The Book of Llandaff tells his story. When he had reigned well and grown old, he handed the crown to his son Meurig and went to live as a hermit among the rocks of Tintern, above the Wye.",
    "Then the Saxons crossed the river into Gwent. An angel came to the old king, the Book says, and told him to go to his people’s help: the enemy would flee at the sight of his face, but he himself would be wounded and die three days later. Tewdrig put on his armour again and rode at the head of Meurig’s army, and at the ford of Tintern the Saxons broke and ran.",
    "He had been struck by a spear. He asked to be carried towards the Severn, and where the cart stopped by a spring of clear water, he died. His son built a church on the spot and gave the land around it to the bishop. The place is Mathern, in Welsh Merthyr Tewdrig, Tewdrig’s martyrdom. In the early 1600s Bishop Francis Godwin of Llandaff, who lived at Mathern, opened the old grave and found the bones of a man with a wound in the skull, and a tablet in the church still tells his story.",
    "Tewdrig is remembered as a king, a saint and a martyr, and his feast day is 1 April. Through his son Meurig and his grandson Athrwys, Welsh tradition holds, he was Arthur’s grandfather."],
   ["Rheolai Tewdrig ap Llywarch Went a Glywysing, teyrnasoedd y de-ddwyrain rhwng afon Tawe ac afon Gwy. Mae Llyfr Llandaf yn adrodd ei hanes. Wedi iddo deyrnasu’n dda a heneiddio, rhoddodd y goron i’w fab Meurig ac aeth i fyw’n feudwy ymhlith creigiau Tyndyrn, uwchben afon Gwy.",
    "Yna croesodd y Saeson yr afon i Went. Daeth angel at yr hen frenin, medd y Llyfr, a dweud wrtho am fynd i helpu ei bobl: byddai’r gelyn yn ffoi o weld ei wyneb, ond câi ef ei hun ei glwyfo a marw dridiau’n ddiweddarach. Gwisgodd Tewdrig ei arfwisg eto a marchogaeth ar flaen byddin Meurig, ac wrth ryd Tyndyrn torrodd y Saeson a ffoi.",
    "Roedd gwaywffon wedi’i daro. Gofynnodd am gael ei gludo tua Hafren, a lle safodd y wagen wrth ffynnon o ddŵr gloyw, bu farw. Cododd ei fab eglwys ar y fan a rhoi’r tir o’i chwmpas i’r esgob. Y lle yw Merthyr Tewdrig, Mathern yn Saesneg. Yn nechrau’r 1600au agorodd yr Esgob Francis Godwin o Landaf, a oedd yn byw ym Merthyr Tewdrig, yr hen fedd a chael esgyrn gŵr â chlwyf yn ei benglog, ac mae llechen yn yr eglwys yn dal i adrodd ei hanes.",
    "Fe’i cofir yn frenin, yn sant ac yn ferthyr, a’i ddydd gŵyl yw 1 Ebrill. Drwy ei fab Meurig a’i ŵyr Athrwys, yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, ef oedd taid Arthur."]),
 P("meurig-ap-tewdrig", "saints", "fl. early 7th century", "Meurig ap Tewdrig", "Meurig ap Tewdrig", "King of Gwent and Glywysing, Arthur’s father", "Brenin Gwent a Glywysing, tad Arthur", "Meurig_ap_Tewdrig", "rulers",
   "King of Gwent and Glywysing, son of Tewdrig the martyr. Welsh tradition holds that his son Athrwys was Arthur, and some read Uthr Bendragon, the name of Arthur’s father in the tales, as Meurig’s title.",
   "Brenin Gwent a Glywysing, mab Tewdrig y merthyr. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, ei fab Athrwys oedd Arthur, ac mae rhai’n darllen Uthr Bendragon, enw tad Arthur yn y chwedlau, fel teitl Meurig.",
   ["Meurig ap Tewdrig ruled the south-east of Wales after his father Tewdrig gave up the crown. It was his army that the old king led to victory over the Saxons at Tintern, and it was Meurig who raised the church at Mathern, Merthyr Tewdrig, where his father died, and gave its lands to the bishop.",
    "No king appears more often in the charters of the Book of Llandaff. Gift after gift of land to the church is made in Meurig’s name, and his son Athrwys stands beside him as a witness. Meurig married Onbrawst, daughter of Gwrgan Fawr, king of Ergyng, and through her the Welsh kingdom of Ergyng, between the Wye and the Monnow, came to his house.",
    "Welsh tradition holds that Athrwys, Meurig’s son, was Arthur. In the tales and in the Welsh Brut, Arthur’s father is Uthr Bendragon. Alan Wilson and Baram Blackett, who made the case for Athrwys the work of their lives, read Uthr Bendragon not as a name but as a title: uthr, ‘terrible’ or ‘wonderful’, and pen dragon, ‘chief dragon’, the war leader of the Britons. In that reading, the father of Arthur and King Meurig ap Tewdrig are one man. Other historians may see it differently."],
   ["Rheolai Meurig ap Tewdrig dde-ddwyrain Cymru wedi i’w dad Tewdrig roi’r goron heibio. Ei fyddin ef a arweiniodd yr hen frenin i fuddugoliaeth dros y Saeson yn Nhyndyrn, a Meurig a gododd yr eglwys ym Merthyr Tewdrig, lle bu farw ei dad, a rhoi ei thiroedd i’r esgob.",
    "Nid oes brenin sy’n ymddangos yn amlach yn siarteri Llyfr Llandaf. Gwneir rhodd ar ôl rhodd o dir i’r eglwys yn enw Meurig, ac mae ei fab Athrwys yn sefyll wrth ei ochr yn dyst. Priododd Meurig ag Onbrawst, merch Gwrgan Fawr, brenin Ergyng, a thrwyddi hi daeth teyrnas Gymreig Ergyng, rhwng Gwy a Mynwy, i’w dŷ.",
    "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, Athrwys fab Meurig oedd Arthur. Yn y chwedlau ac ym Mrut Cymraeg, Uthr Bendragon yw tad Arthur. Mae Alan Wilson a Baram Blackett, a wnaeth yr achos dros Athrwys yn waith eu bywydau, yn darllen Uthr Bendragon nid fel enw ond fel teitl: uthr, ‘ofnadwy’ neu ‘rhyfeddol’, a phen dragon, ‘prif ddraig’, arweinydd rhyfel y Brythoniaid. Yn ôl y darlleniad hwnnw, yr un gŵr yw tad Arthur a’r Brenin Meurig ap Tewdrig. Gall haneswyr eraill weld pethau’n wahanol."]),
 P("onbrawst", "saints", "fl. early 7th century", "Onbrawst", "Onbrawst", "Queen of Gwent and Glywysing, Arthur’s mother", "Brenhines Gwent a Glywysing, mam Arthur", "", "rulers",
   "Daughter of Gwrgan Fawr, king of Ergyng, and wife of King Meurig ap Tewdrig, and the mother Welsh tradition gives to Arthur.",
   "Merch Gwrgan Fawr, brenin Ergyng, a gwraig y Brenin Meurig ap Tewdrig, a’r fam y mae traddodiad Cymru’n ei rhoi i Arthur.",
   ["Onbrawst was the daughter of Gwrgan Fawr, Gwrgan the Great, king of Ergyng, the Welsh kingdom between the Wye and the Monnow that lies today in Herefordshire. Her name is kept in the Book of Llandaff, where her marriage to Meurig ap Tewdrig, king of Gwent and Glywysing, joined the two royal houses of the south-east.",
    "Through Onbrawst, Ergyng came to the house of Meurig, and her son Athrwys was born to rule a kingdom that stretched from the Tawe to beyond the Wye. Welsh tradition holds that Athrwys was Arthur. In the Welsh Brut, Arthur’s mother is called Eigr; those who hold that Arthur was Athrwys give her name, from the charters, as Onbrawst.",
    "Ergyng was the country of Saint Dyfrig, born at Madley, who in Welsh tradition crowned Arthur. It was also the country of Llygad Amr, the spring where the History of the Britons places the grave of Amr, Arthur’s son. Arthur’s mother’s land is full of his story."],
   ["Merch Gwrgan Fawr, brenin Ergyng, oedd Onbrawst. Ergyng oedd y deyrnas Gymreig rhwng afonydd Gwy a Mynwy sydd heddiw yn swydd Henffordd. Cedwir ei henw yn Llyfr Llandaf, lle unodd ei phriodas â Meurig ap Tewdrig, brenin Gwent a Glywysing, ddau dŷ brenhinol y de-ddwyrain.",
    "Drwy Onbrawst daeth Ergyng i dŷ Meurig, ac fe aned ei mab Athrwys i reoli teyrnas a ymestynnai o Dawe hyd y tu hwnt i Wy. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, Athrwys oedd Arthur. Ym Mrut Cymraeg, Eigr yw enw mam Arthur; mae’r rhai sy’n credu mai Athrwys oedd Arthur yn rhoi ei henw, o’r siarteri, fel Onbrawst.",
    "Ergyng oedd gwlad Dyfrig Sant, a aned ym Madle, ac a goronodd Arthur yn ôl traddodiad Cymru. Hi hefyd oedd gwlad Llygad Amr, y ffynnon lle mae Hanes y Brythoniaid yn gosod bedd Amr, mab Arthur. Mae gwlad mam Arthur yn llawn o’i hanes."]),
 P("morgan-mwynfawr", "saints", "fl. late 7th century", "Morgan Mwynfawr", "Morgan Mwynfawr", "King of Glywysing and Gwent, Arthur’s son", "Brenin Glywysing a Gwent, mab Arthur", "Morgan_ab_Athrwys", "rulers",
   "King of Glywysing and Gwent after his father Athrwys, who, Welsh tradition holds, was Arthur. An old tradition gives his name to Morgannwg, Glamorgan.",
   "Brenin Glywysing a Gwent ar ôl ei dad Athrwys, sef Arthur yn ôl traddodiad Cymru. Mae hen draddodiad yn rhoi ei enw i Forgannwg.",
   ["Morgan ab Athrwys, Morgan Mwynfawr, ‘the Generous’ or ‘the Courteous’, reigned over Glywysing and Gwent after his father Athrwys. In the Book of Llandaff he is one of the great givers: more than thirty charters record his gifts of land to the bishops of Llandaff, from the Vale of Glamorgan to Gwent and Ergyng.",
    "Welsh tradition holds that Morgannwg, the land of Morgan, takes its name from him, and so the name Glamorgan, Gwlad Morgan, keeps him alive to this day. The old list of the Thirteen Treasures of the Island of Britain includes the chariot of Morgan Mwynfawr: whoever sat in it, the list says, would be wherever he wished at once.",
    "His son Ithel and Ithel’s sons ruled after him, and the kings of Glywysing and Gwent for three hundred years and more were of his line. If Athrwys was Arthur, as Welsh tradition holds, then Morgan Mwynfawr was Arthur’s son and heir, and the kingdom of Glamorgan was named for Arthur’s son."],
   ["Teyrnasodd Morgan ab Athrwys, Morgan Mwynfawr, dros Glywysing a Gwent ar ôl ei dad Athrwys. Yn Llyfr Llandaf mae’n un o’r rhoddwyr mawr: mae dros ddeg ar hugain o siarteri’n cofnodi ei roddion tir i esgobion Llandaf, o Fro Morgannwg i Went ac Ergyng.",
    "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, ar ei ôl ef yr enwyd Morgannwg, gwlad Morgan, ac felly mae’r enw’n ei gadw’n fyw hyd heddiw. Mae hen restr Tri Thlws ar Ddeg Ynys Prydain yn cynnwys car Morgan Mwynfawr: pwy bynnag a eisteddai ynddo, medd y rhestr, byddai lle bynnag y mynnai ar unwaith.",
    "Teyrnasodd ei fab Ithel a meibion Ithel ar ei ôl, ac roedd brenhinoedd Glywysing a Gwent am dros dri chan mlynedd o’i linach ef. Os Athrwys oedd Arthur, fel y dywed traddodiad Cymru, yna Morgan Mwynfawr oedd mab ac etifedd Arthur, ac ar ôl mab Arthur yr enwyd Morgannwg."]),

 # ---------- Arthur's kin in the Welsh tales ----------
 P("uthr-bendragon", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Uthr Bendragon", "Uthr Bendragon", "Arthur’s father in the Welsh tales", "Tad Arthur yn y chwedlau Cymraeg", "Uther_Pendragon", "rulers",
   "Arthur’s father in the Welsh Brut and the old poems. Welsh tradition holds that Uthr Bendragon, ‘the wonderful chief dragon’, was a title, and some hold that it belonged to King Meurig ap Tewdrig.",
   "Tad Arthur ym Mrut Cymraeg a’r hen gerddi. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, teitl oedd Uthr Bendragon, ‘y brif ddraig ryfeddol’, ac mae rhai’n credu mai’r Brenin Meurig ap Tewdrig a’i daliai.",
   ["Uthr Bendragon is a name of the oldest Welsh poetry. In Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor?, in the Black Book of Carmarthen, Arthur names among his men ‘Mabon son of Modron, servant of Uthr Bendragon’. The Book of Taliesin keeps a poem called Marwnad Uthr Pen, the Death-Song of Uthr, in which the speaker tells of his battles and says he gave a ninth share to the valour of Arthur.",
    "The Triads remember him for his learning. They name the Three Great Enchantments of the Island of Britain, and one of them is the enchantment of Uthr Bendragon, which he taught to Menw son of Teirgwaedd, one of Arthur’s warriors.",
    "In Geoffrey of Monmouth’s History and in the Welsh Brut y Brenhinedd, Uthr is the brother of Emrys Wledig. When a star like a dragon appeared in the sky at Emrys’s death, Uthr had two golden dragons made, and from then on he was called Pendragon, Chief Dragon. He married Eigr, and their son was Arthur.",
    "Welsh tradition holds that Arthur was Athrwys ap Meurig, and those who have set out that case read Uthr Bendragon as a title, ‘the wonderful chief dragon’, the war leader of the Britons, held by Athrwys’s father, King Meurig ap Tewdrig. The red dragon that flies over Wales today is the dragon of the Pendragon."],
   ["Enw o’r farddoniaeth Gymraeg hynaf yw Uthr Bendragon. Yn Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor?, yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin, mae Arthur yn enwi ymhlith ei wŷr ‘Mabon fab Modron, gwas Uthr Bendragon’. Mae Llyfr Taliesin yn cadw cerdd o’r enw Marwnad Uthr Pen, lle mae’r llefarydd yn adrodd am ei frwydrau ac yn dweud iddo roi nawfed ran i wrhydri Arthur.",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn ei gofio am ei ddysg. Maent yn enwi Tri Phrif Hud Ynys Prydain, ac un ohonynt yw hud Uthr Bendragon, a ddysgodd i Fenw fab Teirgwaedd, un o ryfelwyr Arthur.",
    "Yn Hanes Sieffre o Fynwy ac ym Mrut y Brenhinedd, Uthr yw brawd Emrys Wledig. Pan ymddangosodd seren fel draig yn yr awyr adeg marwolaeth Emrys, gwnaeth Uthr ddwy ddraig aur, ac o hynny ymlaen fe’i galwyd yn Bendragon, y Brif Ddraig. Priododd ag Eigr, a’u mab hwy oedd Arthur.",
    "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, Athrwys ap Meurig oedd Arthur, ac mae’r rhai a osododd yr achos hwnnw allan yn darllen Uthr Bendragon fel teitl, ‘y brif ddraig ryfeddol’, arweinydd rhyfel y Brythoniaid, a ddaliai tad Athrwys, y Brenin Meurig ap Tewdrig. Draig y Pendragon yw’r ddraig goch sy’n chwifio dros Gymru heddiw."]),
 P("eigr", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Eigr", "Eigr", "Arthur’s mother in the Welsh tales", "Mam Arthur yn y chwedlau Cymraeg", "Igraine", "heroes",
   "Arthur’s mother in the Welsh Brut, daughter of Amlawdd Wledig in the Welsh pedigrees. Through her, Arthur was cousin to Culhwch and to Saint Illtud.",
   "Mam Arthur ym Mrut Cymraeg, merch Amlawdd Wledig yn yr achau Cymreig. Drwyddi hi roedd Arthur yn gefnder i Gulhwch ac i Illtud Sant.",
   ["In the Welsh Brut y Brenhinedd, Arthur’s mother is Eigr, the most beautiful woman in the Island of Britain. She married Uthr Bendragon, and bore him a son, Arthur, and a daughter, Anna.",
    "The Welsh pedigrees of the heroes, Bonedd yr Arwyr, make Eigr a daughter of Amlawdd Wledig, and so tie Arthur to the great families of Welsh story. Goleuddydd, mother of Culhwch, was another of Amlawdd’s daughters, and that is why Culhwch ac Olwen calls Culhwch Arthur’s cousin. The Life of Saint Illtud makes the saint’s mother a daughter of Anblaud, king of Britain, and calls Illtud Arthur’s cousin too. In the hunt of Twrch Trwyth, Arthur’s uncles, his mother’s brothers, ride with him.",
    "Welsh tradition holds that Arthur was Athrwys ap Meurig, and gives his mother’s name, from the charters of Llandaff, as Onbrawst, daughter of Gwrgan Fawr. Eigr, the mother of Arthur in the Brut, has kept her place in the story of Wales for nine hundred years."],
   ["Ym Mrut y Brenhinedd, Eigr yw mam Arthur, y wraig harddaf yn Ynys Prydain. Priododd ag Uthr Bendragon, a ganed iddynt fab, Arthur, a merch, Anna.",
    "Mae achau Cymraeg yr arwyr, Bonedd yr Arwyr, yn gwneud Eigr yn ferch i Amlawdd Wledig, ac felly’n clymu Arthur wrth deuluoedd mawr chwedlau Cymru. Merch arall i Amlawdd oedd Goleuddydd, mam Culhwch, a dyna pam mae Culhwch ac Olwen yn galw Culhwch yn gefnder i Arthur. Mae Buchedd Illtud Sant yn gwneud mam y sant yn ferch i Anblaud, brenin Prydain, ac yn galw Illtud yntau’n gefnder i Arthur. Yn helfa’r Twrch Trwyth, mae ewythrod Arthur, brodyr ei fam, yn marchogaeth gydag ef.",
    "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, Athrwys ap Meurig oedd Arthur, ac mae’n rhoi enw ei fam, o siarteri Llandaf, fel Onbrawst, merch Gwrgan Fawr. Mae Eigr, mam Arthur yn y Brut, wedi cadw ei lle yn stori Cymru am naw can mlynedd."]),
 P("madog-ap-uthr", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Madog ap Uthr", "Madog ab Uthr", "Arthur’s brother", "Brawd Arthur", "Madoc_ap_Uthyr", "heroes",
   "Son of Uthr Bendragon and brother of Arthur, mourned in a death-song in the Book of Taliesin. His son Eliwlod speaks with Arthur in the old poem of Arthur and the Eagle.",
   "Mab Uthr Bendragon a brawd Arthur, a alarwyd mewn marwnad yn Llyfr Taliesin. Mae ei fab Eliwlod yn ymddiddan ag Arthur yn yr hen gerdd am Arthur a’r Eryr.",
   ["Madog son of Uthr is a hero of the oldest Welsh poetry. The Book of Taliesin, copied in the fourteenth century from far older poems, keeps Marwnad Madog, the Death-Song of Madog. It praises him as a rampart of joy: before he went to his grave, the poet says, Madog was a fortress of plenty, of great deeds and of merriment.",
    "As the son of Uthr Bendragon, Madog was Arthur’s brother. Few of Arthur’s kin are as old in the Welsh record, and the poets who mourned him knew him as a lord in his own right, generous in his hall and brave in battle.",
    "His son Eliwlod is the speaker of one of the most striking of the old Welsh poems, Ymddiddan Arthur a’r Eryr, the Dialogue of Arthur and the Eagle. In it Arthur meets an eagle on the top of an oak, and the eagle tells him that he is Eliwlod, son of Madog son of Uthr: Arthur’s own nephew, come back to give him counsel."],
   ["Arwr o’r farddoniaeth Gymraeg hynaf yw Madog ab Uthr. Mae Llyfr Taliesin, a gopïwyd yn y bedwaredd ganrif ar ddeg o gerddi llawer hŷn, yn cadw Marwnad Madog. Mae’n ei ganmol yn fur llawenydd: cyn iddo fynd i’w fedd, medd y bardd, bu Madog yn gaer o ddigonedd, o gampau ac o lawenydd.",
    "Fel mab Uthr Bendragon, roedd Madog yn frawd i Arthur. Prin yw perthnasau Arthur sydd mor hen yn y cofnod Cymraeg, ac roedd y beirdd a’i galarodd yn ei adnabod yn arglwydd yn ei hawl ei hun, yn hael yn ei neuadd ac yn ddewr mewn brwydr.",
    "Ei fab Eliwlod yw llefarydd un o’r hen gerddi Cymraeg mwyaf trawiadol, Ymddiddan Arthur a’r Eryr. Ynddi mae Arthur yn cwrdd ag eryr ar ben derwen, ac mae’r eryr yn dweud wrtho mai ef yw Eliwlod, mab Madog ab Uthr: nai Arthur ei hun, wedi dod yn ôl i roi cyngor iddo."]),
 P("eliwlod", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Eliwlod ap Madog", "Eliwlod ap Madog", "Arthur’s nephew, the eagle of the poem", "Nai Arthur, eryr y gerdd", "", "heroes",
   "Son of Madog ap Uthr and nephew of Arthur. In the medieval Welsh poem Ymddiddan Arthur a’r Eryr he speaks to Arthur in the form of an eagle.",
   "Mab Madog ab Uthr a nai Arthur. Yn y gerdd Gymraeg ganoloesol Ymddiddan Arthur a’r Eryr mae’n siarad ag Arthur ar ffurf eryr.",
   ["Eliwlod was the son of Madog son of Uthr, and so Arthur’s nephew. He is the eagle of Ymddiddan Arthur a’r Eryr, the Dialogue of Arthur and the Eagle, a medieval Welsh poem in englynion. Arthur sees a great eagle on the top of an oak and asks who it is. The eagle answers that he is Eliwlod, Arthur’s nephew, and the two talk together of courage, of right living and of faith in God.",
    "The poem shows Arthur as the old Welsh tradition knew him: a king who listens, and who takes counsel from his own kin. Eliwlod tells him to put his trust in God and to rule with justice, and Arthur hears him out.",
    "The list of the twenty-four knights of Arthur’s court, kept with the Triads, names Eliwlod son of Madog son of Uthr as one of the Three Golden-Tongued Knights, with Gwalchmai son of Gwyar and Drudwas son of Tryffin: there was no king or lord to whom they came who would not listen to them, so fair was their speech."],
   ["Mab Madog ab Uthr oedd Eliwlod, ac felly’n nai i Arthur. Ef yw eryr Ymddiddan Arthur a’r Eryr, cerdd Gymraeg ganoloesol ar fesur englyn. Mae Arthur yn gweld eryr mawr ar ben derwen ac yn gofyn pwy ydyw. Mae’r eryr yn ateb mai ef yw Eliwlod, nai Arthur, ac mae’r ddau’n ymddiddan am ddewrder, am fyw’n iawn ac am ffydd yn Nuw.",
    "Mae’r gerdd yn dangos Arthur fel yr adwaenai’r hen draddodiad Cymreig ef: brenin sy’n gwrando, ac sy’n derbyn cyngor gan ei deulu ei hun. Mae Eliwlod yn dweud wrtho am ymddiried yn Nuw ac am reoli’n gyfiawn, ac mae Arthur yn gwrando arno.",
    "Mae rhestr pedwar marchog ar hugain llys Arthur, a gedwir gyda’r Trioedd, yn enwi Eliwlod fab Madog ab Uthr yn un o Dri Eurdafodiog Farchog, gyda Gwalchmai fab Gwyar a Drudwas fab Tryffin: nid oedd brenin nac arglwydd y deuent ato na wrandawai arnynt, mor deg oedd eu hymadrodd."]),
 P("gwenhwyfar", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Gwenhwyfar", "Gwenhwyfar", "Arthur’s queen", "Brenhines Arthur", "Guinevere", "heroes",
   "Arthur’s queen, named in Culhwch ac Olwen as the first lady of the Island of Britain. The Triads speak of her, the romances crown her at Caerleon, and Welsh tradition remembers her home on the border at Cnwclas.",
   "Brenhines Arthur, a enwir yn Culhwch ac Olwen yn bennaf rhiain Ynys Prydain. Mae’r Trioedd yn sôn amdani, mae’r rhamantau’n ei choroni yng Nghaerllion, ac mae traddodiad Cymru’n cofio ei chartref ar y ffin yng Nghnwclas.",
   ["Gwenhwyfar is Arthur’s queen in every part of Welsh tradition. Her name begins with gwen, white, fair and blessed. In Culhwch ac Olwen, written down by about 1100, she is among the seven treasures Arthur will give to no one, with his ship, his mantle and his sword, and when Culhwch claims his gift he names her first of the women: ‘Gwenhwyfar, chief lady of this island’.",
    "The Triads honour her as queen of three courts. They name Arthur’s Three Great Queens, and every one of them is called Gwenhwyfar: Gwenhwyfar daughter of Cywryd of Gwent, Gwenhwyfar daughter of Gwythyr son of Greidiol, and Gwenhwyfar daughter of Gogfran the Giant.",
    "In Geoffrey of Monmouth’s History she is the most beautiful woman in the island, of a noble Roman family, and at the great Whitsun crowning at Caerleon on Usk she is crowned in her own church, the church of the holy maidens, while the queens of the kings who came to Arthur’s court walk before her with white doves. In the Welsh romances of Owain, Peredur and Geraint, she holds her court beside Arthur at Caerleon, and the young heroes of the island go out to defend her honour.",
    "Welsh tradition on the border remembers her father as Gogfran Gawr, and his castle at Cnwclas, Knucklas, in the Teme valley of Radnorshire, where, it is told, Arthur came to win her and they were married."],
   ["Gwenhwyfar yw brenhines Arthur ym mhob rhan o draddodiad Cymru. Mae ei henw’n dechrau â gwen, gwyn, teg a bendigaid. Yn Culhwch ac Olwen, a ysgrifennwyd erbyn tua 1100, mae hi ymhlith y saith trysor na rydd Arthur i neb, gyda’i long, ei fantell a’i gleddyf, a phan hawlia Culhwch ei rodd mae’n ei henwi hi’n gyntaf o’r gwragedd: ‘Gwenhwyfar, pennaf rhiain yr ynys hon’.",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn ei hanrhydeddu’n frenhines tair llys. Maent yn enwi Tair Prif Frenhines Arthur, a Gwenhwyfar yw enw pob un ohonynt: Gwenhwyfar ferch Cywryd Gwent, Gwenhwyfar ferch Gwythyr fab Greidiol, a Gwenhwyfar ferch Gogfran Gawr.",
    "Yn Hanes Sieffre o Fynwy hi yw’r wraig harddaf yn yr ynys, o deulu Rhufeinig bonheddig, ac yn y coroni mawr ar y Sulgwyn yng Nghaerllion ar Wysg fe’i coronir yn ei heglwys ei hun, eglwys y gwyryfon sanctaidd, tra bo breninesau’r brenhinoedd a ddaeth i lys Arthur yn cerdded o’i blaen â cholomennod gwynion. Yn rhamantau Cymraeg Owain, Peredur a Geraint, mae hi’n cadw ei llys wrth ochr Arthur yng Nghaerllion, ac mae arwyr ifanc yr ynys yn mynd allan i amddiffyn ei hanrhydedd.",
    "Mae traddodiad Cymru ar y ffin yn cofio ei thad fel Gogfran Gawr, a’i gastell yng Nghnwclas, yn nyffryn Tefeidiad yn Sir Faesyfed, lle, medd y stori, y daeth Arthur i’w hennill ac y priodasant."]),
 P("llacheu", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Llacheu fab Arthur", "Llacheu fab Arthur", "Arthur’s son", "Mab Arthur", "Llacheu", "heroes",
   "Arthur’s son, a warrior beside Cai in the poem Pa Gŵr, and one of the Three Well-Endowed Men of the Island of Britain in the Triads.",
   "Mab Arthur, rhyfelwr wrth ochr Cai yn y gerdd Pa Gŵr, ac un o Dri Deifniog Ynys Prydain yn y Trioedd.",
   ["Llacheu son of Arthur belongs to the oldest layer of Welsh poetry about Arthur. In Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor?, in the Black Book of Carmarthen, Arthur tells the gatekeeper of the deeds of his men, and says that Cai and Llacheu fought battles together, before the pain of blue spears.",
    "In the Dialogue of Gwyddno Garanhir and Gwyn ap Nudd, also in the Black Book, Gwyn tells of the battles he has seen, and among them: ‘I have been where Llacheu was slain, son of Arthur, wondrous in songs, when ravens croaked over blood.’",
    "The Triads count him among the Three Well-Endowed Men of the Island of Britain, Tri Deifniog Ynys Prydain, with Gwalchmai son of Gwyar and Rhiwallon Broom-Hair: men gifted with learning and every noble skill. The poets of the princes knew his name, and in the French romances he lived on as Loholt, Arthur’s son."],
   ["Mae Llacheu fab Arthur yn perthyn i’r haen hynaf o farddoniaeth Gymraeg am Arthur. Yn Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor?, yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin, mae Arthur yn adrodd wrth y porthor am gampau ei wŷr, ac yn dweud i Gai a Llacheu ymladd brwydrau ynghyd, cyn gloes gwaywffyn glas.",
    "Yn Ymddiddan Gwyddno Garanhir a Gwyn ap Nudd, hefyd yn y Llyfr Du, mae Gwyn yn adrodd am y brwydrau a welodd, ac yn eu plith: ‘Mi a fûm lle lladdwyd Llacheu, mab Arthur, uthr yn ei gerddau, pan grawciai brain ar waed.’",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn ei gyfrif ymhlith Tri Deifniog Ynys Prydain, gyda Gwalchmai fab Gwyar a Rhiwallon Wallt Banhadlen: gwŷr a gafodd ddysg a phob dawn fonheddig. Roedd beirdd y tywysogion yn adnabod ei enw, ac yn y rhamantau Ffrangeg bu fyw fel Loholt, mab Arthur."]),
 P("amr", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Amr fab Arthur", "Amr fab Arthur", "Arthur’s son, buried at Llygad Amr", "Mab Arthur, a gladdwyd yn Llygad Amr", "", "heroes",
   "Arthur’s son, whose grave beside the spring of Llygad Amr in Ergyng is one of the Wonders of Britain in the History of the Britons. In the tale of Geraint he serves at Arthur’s court.",
   "Mab Arthur, y mae ei fedd wrth ffynnon Llygad Amr yn Ergyng yn un o Ryfeddodau Prydain yn Hanes y Brythoniaid. Yn chwedl Geraint mae’n gwasanaethu yn llys Arthur.",
   ["The History of the Britons, written in Gwynedd in about 829, ends with a list of the Wonders of Britain, and one of them is the grave of Amr, son of Arthur. It lies, the book says, in the country called Ergyng, beside a spring called Llygad Amr, the Eye of Amr, and the man buried there was Amr, son of Arthur the soldier.",
    "It is a wonder because it will not be measured. ‘Men come to measure the grave,’ the writer says, ‘and sometimes it is six feet long, sometimes nine, sometimes twelve, sometimes fifteen. Whatever length you measure it at one time, you will not find it the same again, and I have tried it myself.’",
    "The spring is still there. It is Gamber Head, the source of the river Gamber, in the old Welsh land of Ergyng in Herefordshire: the country of Arthur’s mother Onbrawst, in Welsh tradition. In the Welsh romance of Geraint son of Erbin, Amhar son of Arthur is one of the four young men who serve at Arthur’s court at Caerleon."],
   ["Mae Hanes y Brythoniaid, a ysgrifennwyd yng Ngwynedd tua 829, yn gorffen â rhestr o Ryfeddodau Prydain, ac un ohonynt yw bedd Amr, mab Arthur. Mae’n gorwedd, medd y llyfr, yn y wlad a elwir Ergyng, wrth ffynnon a elwir Llygad Amr, a’r gŵr a gladdwyd yno oedd Amr, mab Arthur y milwr.",
    "Mae’n rhyfeddod am na ellir ei fesur. ‘Daw dynion i fesur y bedd,’ medd yr awdur, ‘ac weithiau mae’n chwe throedfedd o hyd, weithiau naw, weithiau deuddeg, weithiau pymtheg. Pa hyd bynnag a fesurwch un tro, ni chewch ef yr un fath eto, ac fe’i profais fy hun.’",
    "Mae’r ffynnon yno o hyd. Gamber Head yw hi, tarddiad afon Gamber, yn hen wlad Gymreig Ergyng yn swydd Henffordd: gwlad Onbrawst, mam Arthur, yn ôl traddodiad Cymru. Yn rhamant Gymraeg Geraint fab Erbin, mae Amhar fab Arthur yn un o’r pedwar llanc sy’n gwasanaethu yn llys Arthur yng Nghaerllion."]),
 P("gwydre", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Gwydre fab Arthur", "Gwydre fab Arthur", "Arthur’s son, who fell in the great hunt", "Mab Arthur, a syrthiodd yn yr helfa fawr", "", "heroes",
   "Arthur’s son, who fell at Cwm Cerwyn in the Preseli hills in the hunt of Twrch Trwyth, the greatest chase of Culhwch ac Olwen.",
   "Mab Arthur, a syrthiodd yng Nghwm Cerwyn ym Mynydd Preseli yn helfa’r Twrch Trwyth, yr helfa fwyaf yn Culhwch ac Olwen.",
   ["In Culhwch ac Olwen, the greatest of all the tasks is to hunt Twrch Trwyth, a king turned into a boar, and to take from between his ears the comb, the razor and the shears. Arthur gathers every warrior of the Island of Britain, and the boar comes ashore at Porth Clais by St Davids.",
    "On the Preseli hills, at Cwm Cerwyn, the boar turns and stands at bay, and the best of Arthur’s champions go in against him. There, the tale says, he slew Gwydre son of Arthur. Arthur followed the boar across the whole of the south, through the Tywi valley, Dyffryn Llwchwr and the Black Mountain to the Severn, and drove it at last into the sea off Cornwall.",
    "Foel Cwmcerwyn is the highest top of Mynydd Preseli. Below the hills, in Cwm Gwaun, stand the standing stones called Cerrig Meibion Arthur, the Stones of the Sons of Arthur, and Welsh tradition holds that they mark the sons of Arthur who fell in the hunt."],
   ["Yn Culhwch ac Olwen, y fwyaf o’r holl dasgau yw hela’r Twrch Trwyth, brenin a drowyd yn faedd, a chymryd y grib, yr ellyn a’r gwellau o rhwng ei ddau glust. Mae Arthur yn casglu pob rhyfelwr yn Ynys Prydain, a daw’r twrch i’r lan ym Mhorth Clais ger Tyddewi.",
    "Ar Fynydd Preseli, yng Nghwm Cerwyn, mae’r twrch yn troi ac yn sefyll, ac mae goreuon Arthur yn mynd yn ei erbyn. Yno, medd y chwedl, y lladdodd Wydre fab Arthur. Dilynodd Arthur y twrch ar draws y de i gyd, drwy Ddyffryn Tywi, Dyffryn Llwchwr a’r Mynydd Du hyd at Hafren, a’i yrru o’r diwedd i’r môr ger Cernyw.",
    "Foel Cwmcerwyn yw copa uchaf Mynydd Preseli. Islaw’r bryniau, yng Nghwm Gwaun, saif y meini hirion a elwir Cerrig Meibion Arthur, ac yn ôl traddodiad Cymru maent yn nodi meibion Arthur a syrthiodd yn yr helfa."]),
 P("medrawd", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Medrawd", "Medrawd", "Who fell with Arthur at Camlan", "A syrthiodd gydag Arthur yng Nghamlan", "Mordred", "heroes",
   "Named with Arthur in the Welsh Annals: ‘the strife of Camlan, in which Arthur and Medrawd fell’. In the Welsh Brut he is Arthur’s nephew, brother of Gwalchmai.",
   "Fe’i henwir gydag Arthur ym Mlwyddnodion Cymru: ‘Gwaith Camlan, lle syrthiodd Arthur a Medrawd’. Ym Mrut Cymraeg mae’n nai i Arthur, yn frawd i Walchmai.",
   ["Medrawd is one of only two men named in the Welsh Annals’ entry for Camlan, Arthur’s last battle: ‘The strife of Camlan, in which Arthur and Medrawd fell; and there was plague in Britain and in Ireland.’ The Annals were kept at St Davids and brought together in the tenth century, and they say no more. They do not say on which side Medrawd fought.",
    "In the older Welsh tradition he was remembered with honour. The list of the twenty-four knights of Arthur’s court, kept with the Triads, names Medrawd as one of the Three Royal Knights, with Nasiens son of the king of Denmark and Hywel son of Emyr Llydaw: so fair and wise were they in peace that no king or emperor could refuse them, and in war no warrior could stand against them.",
    "In Geoffrey of Monmouth’s History and the Welsh Brut, Medrawd is Arthur’s nephew, son of his sister Anna, and brother of Gwalchmai. Arthur leaves the island in his care when he crosses the sea, and the two kinsmen meet at last in battle at Camlan. Welsh tradition places Camlan in Meirionnydd, where the stream called Camlan runs down near Dinas Mawddwy, and the farms and fields around still carry names of the battle."],
   ["Medrawd yw un o ddau ŵr yn unig a enwir yng nghofnod Blwyddnodion Cymru am Gamlan, brwydr olaf Arthur: ‘Gwaith Camlan, lle syrthiodd Arthur a Medrawd; a bu haint ym Mhrydain ac yn Iwerddon.’ Cadwyd y Blwyddnodion yn Nhyddewi a’u casglu ynghyd yn y ddegfed ganrif, ac nid ydynt yn dweud mwy. Nid ydynt yn dweud ar ba ochr yr ymladdodd Medrawd.",
    "Yn yr hen draddodiad Cymreig fe’i cofid ag anrhydedd. Mae rhestr pedwar marchog ar hugain llys Arthur, a gedwir gyda’r Trioedd, yn enwi Medrawd yn un o Dri Theyrnaidd Farchog, gyda Nasiens fab brenin Denmarc a Hywel fab Emyr Llydaw: mor deg a doeth oeddynt mewn heddwch na allai brenin nac ymherodr eu gwrthod, ac mewn rhyfel ni allai rhyfelwr sefyll yn eu herbyn.",
    "Yn Hanes Sieffre o Fynwy ac ym Mrut Cymraeg, Medrawd yw nai Arthur, mab ei chwaer Anna, a brawd Gwalchmai. Mae Arthur yn gadael yr ynys yn ei ofal pan groesa’r môr, ac mae’r ddau berthynas yn cwrdd o’r diwedd mewn brwydr yng Nghamlan. Mae traddodiad Cymru’n gosod Camlan ym Meirionnydd, lle mae’r nant a elwir Camlan yn rhedeg i lawr ger Dinas Mawddwy, ac mae’r ffermydd a’r caeau o gwmpas yn dal i ddwyn enwau’r frwydr."]),
 P("gwalchmai", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Gwalchmai fab Gwyar", "Gwalchmai fab Gwyar", "Arthur’s nephew, the hawk of May", "Nai Arthur, hebog Mai", "Gawain", "heroes",
   "Arthur’s nephew, the best on foot and on horseback in Culhwch ac Olwen, who never came home without what he had gone to seek. The French knew him as Gawain.",
   "Nai Arthur, y gorau ar droed ac ar farch yn Culhwch ac Olwen, na ddaeth adref erioed heb yr hyn yr aeth i’w geisio. Gawain oedd enw’r Ffrancwyr arno.",
   ["Gwalchmai, ‘the hawk of May’ or ‘the hawk of the plain’, is Arthur’s nephew, his sister’s son. Culhwch ac Olwen gives him one of its finest lines: ‘He never came home without the quest he had gone to seek. He was the best on foot and the best on horseback. He was Arthur’s nephew, his sister’s son, and his first cousin.’ Arthur chooses him for Culhwch’s company when they set out to find Olwen.",
    "The Triads name him among the Three Well-Endowed Men of the Island of Britain, and the list of Arthur’s twenty-four knights makes him one of the Three Golden-Tongued Knights, whose words no king or lord would refuse. In the three Welsh romances he is the most courteous of Arthur’s men. In Peredur, when the young hero falls into a trance over three drops of blood on the snow, it is Gwalchmai who goes to him gently, where others had gone with harsh words, and brings him back to Arthur’s court.",
    "His grave is in Wales. The Stanzas of the Graves, in the Black Book of Carmarthen, say that ‘the grave of Gwalchmai is in Peryddon’. And the English historian William of Malmesbury wrote in about 1125 that in the days of William the Conqueror the tomb of Walwen, Arthur’s nephew, was found in the province of Rhos in Wales, by the sea, fourteen feet long. Walwyn’s Castle in Pembrokeshire is said to carry his name."],
   ["Gwalchmai, ‘hebog Mai’ neu ‘hebog y maes’, yw nai Arthur, mab ei chwaer. Mae Culhwch ac Olwen yn rhoi iddo un o’i llinellau gorau: ‘Ni ddaeth adref erioed heb y neges yr aeth i’w cheisio. Ef oedd y gorau ar droed a’r gorau ar farch. Nai Arthur oedd ef, mab ei chwaer, a’i gefnder.’ Mae Arthur yn ei ddewis i gwmni Culhwch pan gychwynnant i chwilio am Olwen.",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn ei enwi ymhlith Tri Deifniog Ynys Prydain, ac mae rhestr pedwar marchog ar hugain Arthur yn ei wneud yn un o Dri Eurdafodiog Farchog, na wrthodai brenin nac arglwydd eu geiriau. Yn y tair rhamant Gymraeg ef yw’r mwyaf cwrtais o wŷr Arthur. Yn Peredur, pan syrth yr arwr ifanc i berlewyg uwchben tri dafn o waed ar yr eira, Gwalchmai sy’n mynd ato’n dyner, lle’r aeth eraill â geiriau llym, ac yn dod ag ef yn ôl i lys Arthur.",
    "Mae ei fedd yng Nghymru. Mae Englynion y Beddau, yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin, yn dweud bod ‘bedd Gwalchmai ym Mheryddon’. Ac ysgrifennodd yr hanesydd Seisnig William o Malmesbury tua 1125 i fedd Walwen, nai Arthur, gael ei ddarganfod yn nyddiau Gwilym Goncwerwr yng nghantref Rhos yng Nghymru, ger y môr, yn bedair troedfedd ar ddeg o hyd. Dywedir bod Castell Gwalchmai, Walwyn’s Castle, yn Sir Benfro yn dwyn ei enw."]),
 P("culhwch", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Culhwch", "Culhwch", "Arthur’s cousin, who won Olwen", "Cefnder Arthur, a enillodd Olwen", "Culhwch_and_Olwen", "heroes",
   "Arthur’s cousin and the hero of Culhwch ac Olwen, the oldest full tale of Arthur in any language. With the help of Arthur and his warriors he won Olwen, daughter of the chief giant Ysbaddaden.",
   "Cefnder Arthur ac arwr Culhwch ac Olwen, y stori lawn hynaf am Arthur mewn unrhyw iaith. Gyda help Arthur a’i ryfelwyr enillodd Olwen, merch Ysbaddaden Bencawr.",
   ["Culhwch was the son of Cilydd son of Celyddon Wledig and of Goleuddydd, daughter of Amlawdd Wledig, which made him Arthur’s first cousin. His mother gave birth to him in a pig-run, the tale says, and so he was called Culhwch, from the run, cul, and the sow, hwch.",
    "His stepmother laid a destiny on him: he would never have a wife until he won Olwen, daughter of Ysbaddaden Bencawr, the chief of giants. At the very sound of her name, love for Olwen filled him. His father told him to go to his cousin Arthur, have his hair trimmed by the king, and ask him for her as his gift.",
    "His ride to Arthur’s court is one of the great pictures of Welsh prose: a dappled grey steed four winters old, a gold saddle, two sharpened spears of silver, a gold-hilted sword, two white-breasted greyhounds bounding before him, and his steed so light that not a blade of grass bent beneath its hooves. At the gate he would not wait. He asked his gift of Arthur in the name of more than two hundred of Arthur’s warriors and of the ladies of the island, and Arthur granted it.",
    "Arthur sent with him Cai, Bedwyr, Cynddylig the Guide, Gwrhyr Interpreter of Tongues, Gwalchmai and Menw, and with their help Culhwch met every one of the giant’s impossible tasks. That night Olwen slept with Culhwch, the tale ends, and she was his only wife as long as he lived."],
   ["Mab Cilydd fab Celyddon Wledig a Goleuddydd ferch Amlawdd Wledig oedd Culhwch, a hynny’n ei wneud yn gefnder i Arthur. Rhoddodd ei fam enedigaeth iddo mewn twlc moch, medd y chwedl, ac felly fe’i galwyd yn Gulhwch.",
    "Tynghedodd ei lysfam ef: ni châi fyth wraig nes ennill Olwen, merch Ysbaddaden Bencawr. Wrth glywed ei henw’n unig, llanwyd ef â chariad at Olwen. Dywedodd ei dad wrtho am fynd at ei gefnder Arthur, cael trwsio ei wallt gan y brenin, a gofyn iddo amdani’n rhodd.",
    "Mae ei daith i lys Arthur yn un o luniau mawr rhyddiaith Gymraeg: march brychlwyd pedwar gaeaf oed, cyfrwy aur, dwy waywffon arian hogedig, cleddyf eurddwrn, dau filgi bronwyn yn llamu o’i flaen, a’i farch mor ysgafn fel na phlygai blewyn glaswellt dan ei garnau. Wrth y porth ni fynnai aros. Gofynnodd ei rodd gan Arthur yn enw mwy na dau gant o ryfelwyr Arthur ac o rianedd yr ynys, a rhoddodd Arthur hi iddo.",
    "Anfonodd Arthur gydag ef Gai, Bedwyr, Cynddylig Gyfarwydd, Gwrhyr Gwalstawd Ieithoedd, Gwalchmai a Menw, a chyda’u help cyflawnodd Culhwch bob un o dasgau amhosibl y cawr. Y noson honno cysgodd Olwen gyda Chulhwch, medd diwedd y chwedl, a hi fu ei unig wraig tra bu fyw."]),
 P("olwen", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Olwen", "Olwen", "Daughter of Ysbaddaden, of the white track", "Merch Ysbaddaden, yr ôl gwyn", "Culhwch_and_Olwen", "heroes",
   "The heroine of Culhwch ac Olwen. Four white trefoils sprang up behind her wherever she walked, and so she was called Olwen, ‘white track’.",
   "Arwres Culhwch ac Olwen. Tyfai pedair meillionen wen y tu ôl iddi lle bynnag y cerddai, ac felly y galwyd hi’n Olwen.",
   ["Olwen was the daughter of Ysbaddaden Bencawr, chief of giants, and her coming is the most famous picture in Culhwch ac Olwen. She wore a robe of flame-red silk and a torque of red gold set with pearls and rubies. Her hair was yellower than the flower of the broom, her skin whiter than the foam of the wave, and her cheeks redder than the foxglove. ‘Four white trefoils sprang up behind her wherever she went,’ the tale says, ‘and for that reason she was called Olwen’: ol, track, and gwen, white.",
    "She met Culhwch with wisdom as well as love. She told him that she could not go with him without her father’s word, for her father’s life would last only until she married, and she showed him the way: go to her father and ask for her, and whatever he asks, promise to get it. So began the long list of the giant’s tasks, the anoethau, the things hard to win, and Arthur’s men won every one.",
    "When the last task was done, Olwen became Culhwch’s wife, and the tale says she was his only wife as long as he lived. Olwen has been a Welsh girl’s name ever since, and the white clover, meillion Olwen, is hers."],
   ["Merch Ysbaddaden Bencawr oedd Olwen, ac mae ei dyfodiad yn un o luniau enwocaf Culhwch ac Olwen. Gwisgai grys o sidan fflamgoch a thorch o aur coch am ei gwddf, â mererid a gemau rhuddion arni. Melynach oedd ei phen na blodau’r banadl, gwynnach ei chnawd nag ewyn y don, a chochach ei gruddiau na’r ffion. ‘Pedair meillionen wen a dyfai yn ei hôl lle bynnag y delai,’ medd y chwedl, ‘ac am hynny y gelwid hi Olwen’.",
    "Cyfarfu â Chulhwch â doethineb yn ogystal â chariad. Dywedodd wrtho na allai fynd gydag ef heb air ei thad, gan na fyddai oes ei thad ond hyd nes iddi briodi, a dangosodd y ffordd iddo: dos at fy nhad a gofyn amdanaf, a pha beth bynnag a ofynno, addo ei gael. Felly y dechreuodd rhestr hir tasgau’r cawr, yr anoethau, a gwŷr Arthur a enillodd bob un.",
    "Pan gyflawnwyd y dasg olaf, daeth Olwen yn wraig i Gulhwch, a dywed y chwedl mai hi fu ei unig wraig tra bu fyw. Mae Olwen wedi bod yn enw merch yng Nghymru byth er hynny, ac iddi hi y perthyn y feillionen wen."]),
 P("emrys-wledig", "saints", "fl. 5th century", "Emrys Wledig", "Emrys Wledig", "King of the Britons, Arthur’s uncle in the Brut", "Brenin y Brythoniaid, ewythr Arthur yn y Brut", "Ambrosius_Aurelianus", "rulers",
   "The leader whom Gildas praised for rallying the Britons, Ambrosius Aurelianus. Welsh tradition remembers him as Emrys Wledig, king of the Britons, who gave his name to Dinas Emrys in Eryri. In the Welsh Brut he is Arthur’s uncle.",
   "Yr arweinydd a ganmolwyd gan Gildas am uno’r Brythoniaid, Ambrosius Aurelianus. Mae traddodiad Cymru’n ei gofio fel Emrys Wledig, brenin y Brythoniaid, a roddodd ei enw i Ddinas Emrys yn Eryri. Ym Mrut Cymraeg mae’n ewythr i Arthur.",
   ["Gildas, the Briton who wrote On the Ruin of Britain in about 540, names only one leader of the Britons of the generation before his own with real praise: Ambrosius Aurelianus, ‘a gentle man, who alone of the Roman nation had survived’, whose parents had worn the purple. Under him the Britons took courage, challenged the invaders to battle, and won. From that day, Gildas says, victory went now to one side and now to the other, until the siege of Mount Badon.",
    "Welsh tradition knows him as Emrys Wledig, and calls him a king of the Britons. The History of the Britons, written in Gwynedd in about 829, tells how the boy Emrys was brought before Gwrtheyrn at the fortress in Eryri that would not stand. Emrys told the king why: beneath the hill lay a pool, and in it two dragons, one red and one white. When they were uncovered they fought, and the red dragon of the Britons drove out the white. Gwrtheyrn gave the fortress to the boy, and it has been Dinas Emrys ever since.",
    "In Geoffrey of Monmouth’s History and the Welsh Brut, Emrys is the brother of Uthr Bendragon, and so Arthur’s uncle. He becomes king of the Britons, rebuilds their churches, and raises the great stones of the Giants’ Dance on Salisbury Plain in memory of the Britons who had fallen. Geoffrey gave the boy of Dinas Emrys a new name, Myrddin Emrys, and so Emrys and Myrddin are linked in Welsh tradition for ever."],
   ["Un arweinydd yn unig o’r Brythoniaid yn y genhedlaeth o’i flaen y mae Gildas, y Brython a ysgrifennodd Am Ddinistr Prydain tua 540, yn ei enwi â gwir glod: Ambrosius Aurelianus, ‘gŵr tirion, yr unig un o’r genedl Rufeinig a oroesodd’, y gwisgasai ei rieni’r porffor. Dano ef magodd y Brythoniaid nerth, herio’r goresgynwyr i frwydr, ac ennill. O’r dydd hwnnw, medd Gildas, aeth y fuddugoliaeth weithiau i un ochr ac weithiau i’r llall, hyd at warchae Mynydd Baddon.",
    "Mae traddodiad Cymru’n ei adnabod fel Emrys Wledig, ac yn ei alw’n frenin y Brythoniaid. Mae Hanes y Brythoniaid, a ysgrifennwyd yng Ngwynedd tua 829, yn adrodd sut y dygwyd y bachgen Emrys gerbron Gwrtheyrn wrth y gaer yn Eryri na safai. Dywedodd Emrys wrth y brenin pam: dan y bryn roedd llyn, ac ynddo ddwy ddraig, un goch ac un wen. Pan ddadorchuddiwyd hwy fe ymladdasant, a gyrrodd draig goch y Brythoniaid y wen allan. Rhoddodd Gwrtheyrn y gaer i’r bachgen, a Dinas Emrys fu hi byth er hynny.",
    "Yn Hanes Sieffre o Fynwy ac ym Mrut Cymraeg, Emrys yw brawd Uthr Bendragon, ac felly’n ewythr i Arthur. Daw’n frenin y Brythoniaid, mae’n ailgodi eu heglwysi, ac yn codi meini mawr Côr y Cewri ar Wastadedd Caersallog er cof am y Brythoniaid a syrthiodd. Rhoddodd Sieffre enw newydd i fachgen Dinas Emrys, Myrddin Emrys, ac felly mae Emrys a Myrddin ynghlwm yn nhraddodiad Cymru am byth."]),

 # ---------- Arthur's warband ----------
 P("cai", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Cai fab Cynyr", "Cai fab Cynyr", "Arthur’s first warrior", "Prif ryfelwr Arthur", "Sir_Kay", "heroes",
   "The first of Arthur’s warriors in the oldest Welsh poems and tales: a hero of wonders who could go nine nights without sleep, fought the Cath Palug on Ynys Môn, and rode with Arthur in the Life of Saint Cadog.",
   "Y cyntaf o ryfelwyr Arthur yn yr hen gerddi a’r chwedlau Cymraeg: arwr rhyfeddodau a allai fynd naw nos heb gwsg, a ymladdodd â’r Gath Balug ar Ynys Môn, ac a farchogodd gydag Arthur ym Muchedd Cadog Sant.",
   ["Cai son of Cynyr Ceinfarfog, Cai Wyn, Cai the Fair, is the first of Arthur’s men in the oldest Welsh poetry. In Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor?, Arthur speaks of him again and again: Cai fought nine witches on the heights of Ystafngwn, went to Ynys Môn to destroy monsters, and raised his shield against the Cath Palug, the great cat of Môn that had killed nine score warriors. ‘Vain were they who tested Cai,’ Arthur says, and the poem calls him the fair one, ‘tall in the hall’.",
    "Culhwch ac Olwen gives him the gifts of a hero of wonders. He could hold his breath nine nights and nine days under water, and go nine nights and nine days without sleep. No doctor could heal a wound from his sword. When he wished, he could make himself as tall as the tallest tree in the forest, and when the rain fell hardest, whatever he carried would be dry a hand’s breadth above and below, so great was the heat in him. It is Cai who wins Wrnach the Giant’s sword, and Cai and Bedwyr who pluck the beard of Dillus the Bearded.",
    "The Triads name him with Drystan son of March and Hueil son of Caw as one of the Three Battle-Diademed Men of the Island of Britain. In the Life of Saint Cadog, he and Bedwyr are with Arthur on the hill when Gwynllyw comes riding by with his bride. Caer Gai, the Roman fort above Llyn Tegid near Llanuwchllyn, has carried his name for centuries, and local tradition holds that it was his home."],
   ["Cai fab Cynyr Ceinfarfog, Cai Wyn, yw’r cyntaf o wŷr Arthur yn y farddoniaeth Gymraeg hynaf. Yn Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor?, mae Arthur yn sôn amdano dro ar ôl tro: ymladdodd Cai â naw gwrach ar ucheldir Ystafngwn, aeth i Ynys Môn i ddifa anghenfilod, a chododd ei darian yn erbyn y Gath Balug, cath fawr Môn a laddasai naw ugain o ryfelwyr. ‘Ofer oedd y rhai a brofai Gai,’ medd Arthur, ac mae’r gerdd yn ei alw’n deg, ‘hir yn y neuadd’.",
    "Mae Culhwch ac Olwen yn rhoi iddo ddoniau arwr rhyfeddodau. Gallai ddal ei anadl naw nos a naw dydd dan ddŵr, a mynd naw nos a naw dydd heb gwsg. Ni allai meddyg wella clwyf o’i gleddyf. Pan fynnai, gallai fod mor dal â’r goeden dalaf yn y goedwig, a phan oedd y glaw drymaf, byddai’r hyn a gariai’n sych led llaw uwchlaw ac islaw, mor fawr oedd y gwres ynddo. Cai sy’n ennill cleddyf Wrnach Gawr, a Chai a Bedwyr sy’n tynnu barf Dillus Farfog.",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn ei enwi gyda Drystan fab March a Hueil fab Caw yn un o Dri Thaleithiog Cad Ynys Prydain. Ym Muchedd Cadog Sant, mae ef a Bedwyr gydag Arthur ar y bryn pan ddaw Gwynllyw heibio ar ei farch gyda’i briodferch. Mae Caer Gai, y gaer Rufeinig uwchben Llyn Tegid ger Llanuwchllyn, wedi dwyn ei enw ers canrifoedd, ac yn ôl traddodiad lleol yno oedd ei gartref."]),
 P("bedwyr", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Bedwyr fab Pedrog", "Bedwyr fab Pedrog", "Arthur’s faithful companion", "Cydymaith ffyddlon Arthur", "Bedivere", "heroes",
   "Arthur’s companion from the oldest poems onwards, the most handsome man in the island after Arthur in Culhwch ac Olwen. The Stanzas of the Graves place his grave on the slope of Tryfan.",
   "Cydymaith Arthur o’r hen gerddi ymlaen, y gŵr harddaf yn yr ynys ar ôl Arthur yn Culhwch ac Olwen. Mae Englynion y Beddau’n gosod ei fedd ar lethr Tryfan.",
   ["Bedwyr son of Pedrog is always at Arthur’s side. In Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor? he fights on the shores of Tryfrwyd, and the poem calls him Bedwyr Bedrydant, Bedwyr of the perfect sinews. In Culhwch ac Olwen he goes wherever Cai goes, and the tale says that no one in the island was as handsome as he, save only Arthur and Drych son of Cibddar. Though he had only one hand, no three warriors on a field of battle drew blood faster than he, and his spear made one wound going in and nine coming out.",
    "The Triads set him above the three greatest fighters of the island. After naming the Three Battle-Diademed Men, Drystan, Hueil and Cai, they add: ‘and one was diademed above the three: Bedwyr son of Pedrog’. In the Life of Saint Cadog he is with Arthur and Cai on the hill above Gwynllŵg. In Geoffrey of Monmouth’s History he is Arthur’s cup-bearer, and goes with him alone to fight the giant of Mont-Saint-Michel.",
    "The Stanzas of the Graves, in the Black Book of Carmarthen, say: ‘the grave of Bedwyr is on the slope of Tryfan’. In Eryri, Welsh tradition holds that after Arthur’s last battle at Bwlch y Saethau it was Bedwyr who carried Caledfwlch down the mountain and threw it into Llyn Llydaw, and a hand rose from the water to take it."],
   ["Mae Bedwyr fab Pedrog bob amser wrth ochr Arthur. Yn Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor? mae’n ymladd ar lannau Tryfrwyd, ac mae’r gerdd yn ei alw’n Fedwyr Bedrydant. Yn Culhwch ac Olwen mae’n mynd lle bynnag yr â Chai, ac medd y chwedl nad oedd neb yn yr ynys mor hardd ag ef, ond Arthur a Drych fab Cibddar yn unig. Er mai un llaw oedd ganddo, ni thynnai tri rhyfelwr ar faes y gad waed yn gynt nag ef, a gwnâi ei waywffon un archoll wrth fynd i mewn a naw wrth ddod allan.",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn ei osod uwchlaw tri ymladdwr mwyaf yr ynys. Wedi enwi Tri Thaleithiog Cad, Drystan, Hueil a Chai, maent yn ychwanegu: ‘ac un oedd yn daleithiog uwchben y tri: Bedwyr fab Pedrog’. Ym Muchedd Cadog Sant mae gydag Arthur a Chai ar y bryn uwchben Gwynllŵg. Yn Hanes Sieffre o Fynwy, ef yw trulliad Arthur, ac mae’n mynd gydag ef ei hunan i ymladd â chawr Mont-Saint-Michel.",
    "Mae Englynion y Beddau, yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin, yn dweud: ‘bedd Bedwyr yn allt Tryfan’. Yn Eryri, yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, wedi brwydr olaf Arthur ym Mwlch y Saethau, Bedwyr a gariodd Galedfwlch i lawr y mynydd a’i daflu i Lyn Llydaw, a chododd llaw o’r dŵr i’w gymryd."]),
 P("glewlwyd", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Glewlwyd Gafaelfawr", "Glewlwyd Gafaelfawr", "Arthur’s gatekeeper", "Porthor Arthur", "Glewlwyd_Gafaelfawr", "heroes",
   "Arthur’s gatekeeper, Glewlwyd Mighty-Grasp, who guards the door in the poem Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor? and in Culhwch ac Olwen, and who had been, he said, to every land in the world.",
   "Porthor Arthur, Glewlwyd Gafaelfawr, sy’n gwarchod y drws yn y gerdd Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor? ac yn Culhwch ac Olwen, ac a fu, medd ef, ym mhob gwlad yn y byd.",
   ["The oldest poem about Arthur’s men begins at a gate. ‘What man is the gatekeeper?’ asks Arthur in Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor?, in the Black Book of Carmarthen. ‘Glewlwyd Gafaelfawr,’ comes the answer. ‘What man asks it?’ ‘Arthur and Cai the fair.’ And before Glewlwyd will open, Arthur must tell him who his warriors are and what they have done, and so the poem becomes a roll of honour of the heroes of Britain.",
    "In Culhwch ac Olwen, Glewlwyd keeps Arthur’s gate on the first day of each January. When young Culhwch rides up, Glewlwyd goes in to tell Arthur that he has never seen a man so fine. ‘I have been in Caer Se and Asse, in Sach and Salach,’ he says, ‘I was in India the Great and India the Less, in Europe and Africa and the islands of Corsica; I have seen kings of renown, and never one as handsome as the man at the gate.’",
    "In the Welsh romances of Owain and Geraint, Glewlwyd is still at Arthur’s court at Caerleon, now honoured as the officer who welcomes guests and strangers and brings them in to the king."],
   ["Mae’r gerdd hynaf am wŷr Arthur yn dechrau wrth borth. ‘Pa ŵr yw’r porthor?’ medd Arthur yn Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor?, yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin. ‘Glewlwyd Gafaelfawr,’ yw’r ateb. ‘Pa ŵr a’i gofyn?’ ‘Arthur a Chai Wyn.’ A chyn yr agora Glewlwyd, rhaid i Arthur ddweud wrtho pwy yw ei ryfelwyr a beth a wnaethant, ac felly daw’r gerdd yn rhestr anrhydedd arwyr Prydain.",
    "Yn Culhwch ac Olwen, Glewlwyd sy’n cadw porth Arthur ar ddydd cyntaf pob Ionawr. Pan ddaw Culhwch ifanc at y porth, mae Glewlwyd yn mynd i mewn i ddweud wrth Arthur na welodd erioed ŵr mor hardd. ‘Bûm yng Nghaer Se ac Asse, yn Sach a Salach,’ medd ef, ‘bûm yn India Fawr ac India Fechan, yn Ewrop ac Affrica ac ynysoedd Corsica; gwelais frenhinoedd enwog, ac ni welais erioed un mor hardd â’r gŵr wrth y porth.’",
    "Yn rhamantau Cymraeg Owain a Geraint, mae Glewlwyd o hyd yn llys Arthur yng Nghaerllion, wedi’i anrhydeddu’n swyddog sy’n croesawu gwesteion a dieithriaid ac yn eu dwyn i mewn at y brenin."]),
 P("mabon", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Mabon fab Modron", "Mabon fab Modron", "The prisoner freed by Arthur’s men", "Y carcharor a ryddhawyd gan wŷr Arthur", "Mabon_ap_Modron", "heroes",
   "The hunter taken from his mother when three nights old and freed from his prison at Caerloyw by Arthur’s men, after a search that went from the oldest creatures of the world to the salmon of Llyn Llyw.",
   "Yr heliwr a ddygwyd oddi wrth ei fam yn dair nos oed ac a ryddhawyd o’i garchar yng Nghaerloyw gan wŷr Arthur, wedi chwilio a aeth o greaduriaid hynaf y byd hyd eog Llyn Llyw.",
   ["Mabon son of Modron is among Arthur’s men in Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor?, where he is called ‘servant of Uthr Bendragon’. His name means simply Son, son of Mother, and it is very old: in Roman times the Britons of the North honoured a Maponos, and his name is carved on altars near Hadrian’s Wall.",
    "In Culhwch ac Olwen, no one can hunt Twrch Trwyth without Mabon, but he was taken from between his mother and the wall when he was three nights old, and no one knows where he is. So begins one of the loveliest passages in Welsh prose, the search for Mabon through the Oldest Animals. Gwrhyr Interpreter of Tongues asks the Ousel of Cilgwri, the Stag of Rhedynfre, the Owl of Cwm Cawlwyd and the Eagle of Gwernabwy, each older than the last, and none has heard of him, until the Eagle leads them to the Salmon of Llyn Llyw, the oldest creature of all.",
    "The Salmon carries Cai and Gwrhyr on his shoulders up the Severn to the walls of Caerloyw, Gloucester, and there they hear Mabon lamenting in his prison. Arthur’s warriors storm the fortress and Cai carries Mabon out on his back. Freed, Mabon rides into the Severn on his horse Gwyn Myngddwn and takes the razor from Twrch Trwyth. The Triads name him one of the Three Exalted Prisoners of the Island of Britain."],
   ["Mae Mabon fab Modron ymhlith gwŷr Arthur yn Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor?, lle’i gelwir yn ‘was Uthr Bendragon’. Ystyr ei enw yw Mab, mab Mam, ac mae’n hen iawn: yn oes y Rhufeiniaid anrhydeddai Brythoniaid y Gogledd un o’r enw Maponos, ac mae ei enw wedi’i naddu ar allorau ger Mur Hadrian.",
    "Yn Culhwch ac Olwen, ni all neb hela’r Twrch Trwyth heb Fabon, ond fe’i dygwyd o rhwng ei fam a’r pared pan oedd yn dair nos oed, ac ni ŵyr neb ble mae. Felly y dechreua un o’r darnau tlysaf mewn rhyddiaith Gymraeg, y chwilio am Fabon drwy’r Anifeiliaid Hynaf. Mae Gwrhyr Gwalstawd Ieithoedd yn holi Mwyalch Cilgwri, Carw Rhedynfre, Tylluan Cwm Cawlwyd ac Eryr Gwernabwy, pob un yn hŷn na’r llall, ac nid oes un wedi clywed amdano, nes i’r Eryr eu harwain at Eog Llyn Llyw, yr hynaf o’r cwbl.",
    "Mae’r Eog yn cario Cai a Gwrhyr ar ei ysgwyddau i fyny Hafren at furiau Caerloyw, ac yno clywant Fabon yn cwyno yn ei garchar. Mae rhyfelwyr Arthur yn ymosod ar y gaer, a Chai yn cario Mabon allan ar ei gefn. Wedi’i ryddhau, mae Mabon yn marchogaeth i Hafren ar ei farch Gwyn Myngddwn ac yn cipio’r ellyn oddi ar y Twrch Trwyth. Mae’r Trioedd yn ei enwi’n un o Dri Goruchel Garcharor Ynys Prydain."]),
 P("caradog-freichfras", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Caradog Freichfras", "Caradog Freichfras", "King of Gwent, Arthur’s chief elder", "Brenin Gwent, pen henuriad Arthur", "Caradoc_Vreichvras", "rulers",
   "Caradog Strong-Arm, king of Gwent in the Welsh pedigrees, named in the very first of the Triads as chief elder of Arthur’s court at Celliwig, and Arthur’s chief counsellor in The Dream of Rhonabwy.",
   "Caradog Freichfras, brenin Gwent yn yr achau Cymreig, a enwir yn y cyntaf un o’r Trioedd yn ben henuriad llys Arthur yng Nghelliwig, a phrif gynghorwr Arthur yn Breuddwyd Rhonabwy.",
   ["The first of the Triads names Arthur’s three royal courts. At Mynyw, Arthur is chief king with Dewi as chief bishop and Maelgwn Gwynedd as chief elder. At Celliwig in Cernyw, Arthur is chief king, Bishop Bytwini chief bishop, and Caradog Freichfras chief elder. Breichfras means ‘strong-armed’, and his name went into French romance as Caradoc Briefbras.",
    "The Welsh pedigrees make him a king of Gwent, son of Llŷr Marini, and an ancestor of the kings who ruled Gwent after him, and Welsh tradition holds that he ruled from Caerwent, the old Roman capital of the Silures. The Triads name his horse Lluagor, ‘host-splitter’, as one of the Three Battle-Horses of the Island of Britain, and his wife was Tegau Eurfron, one of the three splendid maidens of Arthur’s court.",
    "In Breuddwyd Rhonabwy, The Dream of Rhonabwy, Caradog Freichfras is Arthur’s chief counsellor and his cousin. When the host is gathered at the Severn on the eve of Badon, it is Caradog who speaks out plainly to the king, as a trusted elder may, and it is on his word that Arthur leads his army on to Badon."],
   ["Mae’r cyntaf o’r Trioedd yn enwi tair llys frenhinol Arthur. Ym Mynyw, Arthur yw pen teyrn, Dewi yn ben esgob a Maelgwn Gwynedd yn ben henuriad. Yng Nghelliwig yng Nghernyw, Arthur yw pen teyrn, yr Esgob Bytwini yn ben esgob, a Charadog Freichfras yn ben henuriad. Ystyr Breichfras yw ‘â breichiau cryfion’, ac aeth ei enw i’r rhamantau Ffrangeg fel Caradoc Briefbras.",
    "Mae’r achau Cymreig yn ei wneud yn frenin Gwent, mab Llŷr Marini, ac yn gyndad i’r brenhinoedd a reolodd Went ar ei ôl, ac yn ôl traddodiad Cymru rheolai o Gaer-went, hen brifddinas Rufeinig y Silwriaid. Mae’r Trioedd yn enwi ei farch Lluagor yn un o Dri Chadfarch Ynys Prydain, a’i wraig oedd Tegau Eurfron, un o dair rhiain ardderchog llys Arthur.",
    "Yn Breuddwyd Rhonabwy, Caradog Freichfras yw prif gynghorwr Arthur a’i gefnder. Pan gesglir y llu wrth Hafren ar drothwy Baddon, Caradog sy’n siarad yn blaen wrth y brenin, fel y gall henuriad a ymddiriedir, ac ar ei air ef yr arweinia Arthur ei fyddin ymlaen i Faddon."]),
 P("tegau-eurfron", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Tegau Eurfron", "Tegau Eurfron", "The faithful lady of Arthur’s court", "Rhiain ffyddlon llys Arthur", "Tegau_Eurfron", "heroes",
   "Wife of Caradog Freichfras and one of the Three Splendid Maidens of Arthur’s court in the Triads. Her mantle was one of the Thirteen Treasures of the Island of Britain, and for centuries the Welsh poets praised faithful women as ‘a second Tegau’.",
   "Gwraig Caradog Freichfras ac un o Dair Rhiain Ardderchog Llys Arthur yn y Trioedd. Roedd ei mantell yn un o Dri Thlws ar Ddeg Ynys Prydain, ac am ganrifoedd canmolai beirdd Cymru wragedd ffyddlon fel ‘ail Degau’.",
   ["Tegau Eurfron, Tegau Gold-Breast, was the wife of Caradog Freichfras, king of Gwent and chief elder of Arthur’s court. The Triads name her, with Dyfr Golden-Hair and Enid daughter of Ynywl, as one of the Three Splendid Maidens of Arthur’s court.",
    "Her mantle is one of the Thirteen Treasures of the Island of Britain. It would not fit any woman who had been false to her husband, the list says, but it fitted Tegau, and so the mantle of Tegau became the sign of a faithful wife. A French romance tells how her husband was gripped by an enchanted serpent, and Tegau freed him at the cost of part of her own body, which was made good with gold: that, the story says, is how she came to be called Eurfron.",
    "From the fourteenth century to the seventeenth, the Welsh poets of the gentry praised the noblewomen of Wales by comparing them to Tegau. To be called ‘a second Tegau’ was to be praised for beauty, faith and goodness all at once."],
   ["Tegau Eurfron oedd gwraig Caradog Freichfras, brenin Gwent a phen henuriad llys Arthur. Mae’r Trioedd yn ei henwi, gyda Dyfr Wallt Euraid ac Enid ferch Ynywl, yn un o Dair Rhiain Ardderchog Llys Arthur.",
    "Mae ei mantell yn un o Dri Thlws ar Ddeg Ynys Prydain. Ni weddai i unrhyw wraig a fu’n anffyddlon i’w gŵr, medd y rhestr, ond fe weddai i Degau, ac felly daeth mantell Tegau yn arwydd gwraig ffyddlon. Mae rhamant Ffrangeg yn adrodd sut y daliwyd ei gŵr gan sarff hudol, ac i Degau ei ryddhau ar draul rhan o’i chorff ei hun, a wnaed yn iawn ag aur: dyna, medd y stori, sut y cafodd yr enw Eurfron.",
    "O’r bedwaredd ganrif ar ddeg i’r ail ar bymtheg, canmolai beirdd yr uchelwyr foneddigesau Cymru drwy eu cymharu â Thegau. Cael eich galw’n ‘ail Degau’ oedd cael eich canmol am harddwch, ffydd a daioni ar unwaith."]),
 P("gwrhyr", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Gwrhyr Gwalstawd Ieithoedd", "Gwrhyr Gwalstawd Ieithoedd", "Arthur’s interpreter of tongues", "Cyfieithydd ieithoedd Arthur", "", "heroes",
   "Arthur’s interpreter, who knew every language, of men and of birds and beasts. In Culhwch ac Olwen he speaks with the Oldest Animals to find Mabon, and with the young boars of Twrch Trwyth.",
   "Cyfieithydd Arthur, a wyddai bob iaith, iaith dynion ac iaith adar ac anifeiliaid. Yn Culhwch ac Olwen mae’n siarad â’r Anifeiliaid Hynaf i ddod o hyd i Fabon, ac â moch ifanc y Twrch Trwyth.",
   ["Gwrhyr Gwalstawd Ieithoedd, Gwrhyr Interpreter of Tongues, is one of the six whom Arthur sends with Culhwch to find Olwen. ‘He knew every language,’ says Culhwch ac Olwen, and the tale means every one: the languages of the birds and beasts as well as of men.",
    "It is Gwrhyr who leads the search for Mabon son of Modron. He speaks to the Ousel of Cilgwri, who has worn a smith’s anvil down to a nut by pecking at it every evening; to the Stag of Rhedynfre, who has seen an oak grow from a sapling, stand for a hundred years and wither to a stump; to the Owl of Cwm Cawlwyd, who has seen a whole forest grow and fall three times; to the Eagle of Gwernabwy; and at last to the Salmon of Llyn Llyw, who carries him up the Severn to Mabon’s prison.",
    "In the hunt of Twrch Trwyth, Arthur sends Gwrhyr in the shape of a bird to speak with the boar and his young. Grugyn Silver-Bristle answers him, and Gwrhyr carries their words back to Arthur. In the Welsh romances of Geraint and Owain, Gwrhyr is still one of the counsellors at Arthur’s court."],
   ["Mae Gwrhyr Gwalstawd Ieithoedd yn un o’r chwech a anfona Arthur gyda Chulhwch i chwilio am Olwen. ‘Yr oedd ganddo bob iaith,’ medd Culhwch ac Olwen, ac mae’r chwedl yn golygu pob un: ieithoedd yr adar a’r anifeiliaid yn ogystal ag ieithoedd dynion.",
    "Gwrhyr sy’n arwain y chwilio am Fabon fab Modron. Mae’n siarad â Mwyalch Cilgwri, a dreuliodd eingion gof hyd at faint cneuen drwy ei phigo bob noswaith; â Charw Rhedynfre, a welodd dderwen yn tyfu o gangen, yn sefyll am gan mlynedd ac yn gwywo’n fonyn; â Thylluan Cwm Cawlwyd, a welodd goedwig gyfan yn tyfu ac yn syrthio deirgwaith; ag Eryr Gwernabwy; ac o’r diwedd ag Eog Llyn Llyw, sy’n ei gario i fyny Hafren at garchar Mabon.",
    "Yn helfa’r Twrch Trwyth, mae Arthur yn anfon Gwrhyr ar lun aderyn i siarad â’r twrch a’i foch. Grugyn Gwrych Ereint sy’n ei ateb, ac mae Gwrhyr yn dwyn eu geiriau’n ôl at Arthur. Yn rhamantau Cymraeg Geraint ac Owain, mae Gwrhyr o hyd yn un o gynghorwyr llys Arthur."]),
 P("menw", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Menw fab Teirgwaedd", "Menw fab Teirgwaedd", "Arthur’s enchanter", "Swynwr Arthur", "", "heroes",
   "The enchanter of Arthur’s court, who learned his art from Uthr Bendragon. On the quest for Olwen he could cast a spell so that no one saw Arthur’s men while they could see everyone.",
   "Swynwr llys Arthur, a ddysgodd ei gelfyddyd gan Uthr Bendragon. Ar yr helfa am Olwen gallai fwrw hud fel na welai neb wŷr Arthur tra gwelent hwy bawb.",
   ["Menw son of Teirgwaedd is one of the six companions Arthur sends with Culhwch. ‘If they came to a heathen land,’ says Culhwch ac Olwen, ‘he could cast a spell over them so that no one would see them, and they would see everyone.’",
    "The Triads tell where his art came from. They name the Three Great Enchantments of the Island of Britain, and one of them is the enchantment of Uthr Bendragon, which Uthr taught to Menw son of Teirgwaedd. So the enchanter of Arthur’s war band learned his craft from Arthur’s own father.",
    "In the hunt of Twrch Trwyth, it is Menw who goes in the shape of a bird to see whether the treasures are truly between the boar’s ears. He swoops down to snatch one of them, and comes away with only a bristle, and with a wound from the boar’s poison that he carried ever after. Menw is named among the counsellors of Arthur in the Welsh romances too."],
   ["Mae Menw fab Teirgwaedd yn un o’r chwe chydymaith a anfona Arthur gyda Chulhwch. ‘Pe deuent i wlad anghred,’ medd Culhwch ac Olwen, ‘gallai fwrw hud arnynt fel na welai neb hwy, a gwelent hwy bawb.’",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn dweud o ble y daeth ei gelfyddyd. Maent yn enwi Tri Phrif Hud Ynys Prydain, ac un ohonynt yw hud Uthr Bendragon, a ddysgodd Uthr i Fenw fab Teirgwaedd. Felly dysgodd swynwr gosgordd Arthur ei grefft gan dad Arthur ei hun.",
    "Yn helfa’r Twrch Trwyth, Menw sy’n mynd ar lun aderyn i weld a yw’r tlysau yn wir rhwng clustiau’r twrch. Mae’n disgyn i gipio un ohonynt, ac yn dod oddi yno â blewyn yn unig, ac â chlwyf o wenwyn y twrch a gariodd byth wedyn. Enwir Menw ymhlith cynghorwyr Arthur yn y rhamantau Cymraeg hefyd."]),
 P("owain-ab-urien", "saints", "fl. late 6th century", "Owain ab Urien", "Owain ab Urien", "King of Rheged, the knight of the fountain", "Brenin Rheged, marchog y ffynnon", "Owain_mab_Urien", "rulers",
   "King of Rheged after his father Urien, mourned by Taliesin. In Welsh story he became one of Arthur’s greatest knights: the hero of the romance Owain and the lord of the ravens in The Dream of Rhonabwy.",
   "Brenin Rheged ar ôl ei dad Urien, a alarwyd gan Daliesin. Yn chwedlau Cymru daeth yn un o farchogion mwyaf Arthur: arwr rhamant Owain ac arglwydd y brain yn Breuddwyd Rhonabwy.",
   ["Owain son of Urien was a king of Rheged in the Old North, the Welsh-speaking kingdom around the Solway, in the late sixth century. He fought beside his father against the Angles, and Taliesin, his father’s poet, made his elegy. ‘The soul of Owain son of Urien, may the Lord look to its need,’ it begins, and it tells how Owain struck down Fflamddwyn, the Flame-Bearer, the enemy leader, ‘as easily as sleep’.",
    "The Triads count Owain and his twin sister Morfudd, children of Urien and Modron, as one of the Three Fair Womb-Burdens of the Island of Britain. A Life of Saint Kentigern, the saint of Glasgow and of Llanelwy, names the saint’s father as Ewen son of Ulien, and Welsh tradition takes him to be Owain.",
    "Welsh story made him one of Arthur’s greatest knights. In the romance of Owain, also called Iarlles y Ffynnon, The Lady of the Fountain, he rides out from Arthur’s court at Caerleon, wins the knight’s fountain in the enchanted forest and marries its lady, and is saved from death by the wise Luned and later by a lion who follows him like a hound. In Breuddwyd Rhonabwy he plays gwyddbwyll with Arthur on the eve of Badon while his three hundred ravens fight Arthur’s young men. As Yvain, he became a hero across Europe."],
   ["Brenin Rheged yn yr Hen Ogledd oedd Owain ab Urien, y deyrnas Gymraeg ei hiaith o gwmpas Solway, ar ddiwedd y chweched ganrif. Ymladdodd wrth ochr ei dad yn erbyn yr Eingl, a lluniodd Taliesin, bardd ei dad, ei farwnad. ‘Enaid Owain ab Urien, gobwyllid ei Rên o’i raid,’ yw ei dechrau, ac mae’n adrodd sut y trawodd Owain Fflamddwyn, arweinydd y gelyn, i lawr ‘fel cysgu’.",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn cyfrif Owain a’i efaill Morfudd, plant Urien a Modron, yn un o Dri Gwyndorllwyth Ynys Prydain. Mae Buchedd i Sant Cyndeyrn, sant Glasgow a Llanelwy, yn enwi tad y sant yn Ewen fab Ulien, ac mae traddodiad Cymru’n deall mai Owain oedd ef.",
    "Gwnaeth chwedlau Cymru ef yn un o farchogion mwyaf Arthur. Yn rhamant Owain, neu Iarlles y Ffynnon, mae’n marchogaeth allan o lys Arthur yng Nghaerllion, yn ennill ffynnon y marchog yn y goedwig hud ac yn priodi ei harglwyddes, ac fe’i hachubir rhag angau gan Luned ddoeth ac yn ddiweddarach gan lew sy’n ei ddilyn fel ci. Yn Breuddwyd Rhonabwy mae’n chwarae gwyddbwyll gydag Arthur ar drothwy Baddon tra bo ei dri chan brân yn ymladd â gweision ifanc Arthur. Fel Yvain, daeth yn arwr ar draws Ewrop."]),
 P("peredur", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Peredur fab Efrog", "Peredur fab Efrog", "Hero of the romance Peredur", "Arwr rhamant Peredur", "Peredur_son_of_Efrawg", "heroes",
   "The seventh son of Efrog, raised in the wilderness, who rode to Arthur’s court and became one of its greatest knights. His tale, Peredur, is one of the three Welsh romances; the French knew him as Perceval.",
   "Seithfed mab Efrog, a fagwyd yn y diffeithwch, a farchogodd i lys Arthur a dod yn un o’i farchogion mwyaf. Ei chwedl, Peredur, yw un o’r tair rhamant Gymraeg; Perceval oedd enw’r Ffrancwyr arno.",
   ["Peredur was the seventh son of Efrog, an earl of the North. His father and six brothers fell in battle, and his mother took him away to the wilderness to raise him far from war. But one day he saw three of Arthur’s knights ride by, and took them for angels, and nothing would keep him at home. He rode to Arthur’s court on a bony nag, with a pack-saddle for a saddle and a bundle of sharpened sticks for spears.",
    "The tale of his rise is the romance Historia Peredur fab Efrog, one of the three Welsh romances of Arthur’s court. He avenges an insult to Gwenhwyfar on his very first day, learns arms from the witches of Caerloyw, and wins the love of the Empress of Constantinople. In his uncle’s hall he sees a great spear carried past with three streams of blood running from it, and a head on a platter, and the quest of his life is to learn their meaning. In one of the finest scenes in Welsh prose he stops before three drops of blood on the snow, beside a black raven, and falls into a trance thinking of the woman he loves most, until Gwalchmai comes gently to wake him.",
    "The Welsh Annals record that in 580 Gwrgi and Peredur, sons of Eliffer, kings of the Old North, died, and many have seen in that Peredur the first of the name. The French poet Chrétien de Troyes told the same story as Perceval, and from it grew the quest for the Grail."],
   ["Seithfed mab Efrog, iarll yn y Gogledd, oedd Peredur. Syrthiodd ei dad a’i chwe brawd mewn brwydr, ac aeth ei fam ag ef i’r diffeithwch i’w fagu ymhell o ryfel. Ond un diwrnod gwelodd dri o farchogion Arthur yn marchogaeth heibio, a thybio mai angylion oeddynt, ac ni allai dim ei gadw gartref. Marchogodd i lys Arthur ar geffyl esgyrnog, â phynnau’n gyfrwy a sypyn o ffyn hogedig yn waywffyn.",
    "Hanes ei godiad yw’r rhamant Historia Peredur fab Efrog, un o dair rhamant Gymraeg llys Arthur. Mae’n dial sarhad ar Wenhwyfar ar ei ddiwrnod cyntaf, yn dysgu arfau gan wrachod Caerloyw, ac yn ennill cariad Ymerodres Caergystennin. Yn neuadd ei ewythr mae’n gweld gwaywffon fawr yn cael ei chario heibio â thair ffrwd o waed yn rhedeg ohoni, a phen ar ddysgl, a chwest ei fywyd yw dysgu eu hystyr. Yn un o olygfeydd gorau rhyddiaith Gymraeg mae’n aros o flaen tri dafn o waed ar yr eira, wrth ymyl brân ddu, ac yn syrthio i berlewyg wrth feddwl am y wraig a garai fwyaf, nes i Walchmai ddod yn dyner i’w ddeffro.",
    "Mae Blwyddnodion Cymru’n cofnodi i Wrgi a Pheredur, meibion Eliffer, brenhinoedd yr Hen Ogledd, farw yn 580, ac mae llawer wedi gweld yn y Peredur hwnnw y cyntaf o’r enw. Adroddodd y bardd Ffrangeg Chrétien de Troyes yr un stori fel Perceval, ac ohoni y tyfodd y cwest am y Greal."]),
 P("geraint-fab-erbin", "saints", "fl. 6th century", "Geraint fab Erbin", "Geraint fab Erbin", "King of Dyfnaint, hero of Geraint and Enid", "Brenin Dyfnaint, arwr Geraint ac Enid", "Geraint", "rulers",
   "King of Dyfnaint, the land of the Britons of Devon and Cornwall, mourned in the elegy that says ‘In Llongborth I saw Arthur’. The romance Geraint son of Erbin tells how he won Enid.",
   "Brenin Dyfnaint, gwlad Brythoniaid Dyfnaint a Chernyw, a alarwyd yn y farwnad sy’n dweud ‘Yn Llongborth y gwelais i Arthur’. Mae rhamant Geraint fab Erbin yn adrodd sut yr enillodd Enid.",
   ["Geraint son of Erbin was a king of Dyfnaint, the kingdom of the Britons of the south-west, in Devon and Cornwall, in the days when the Britons still held the whole west of the island. The Welsh pedigrees set him in the royal line of Dyfnaint, son of Erbin son of Custennin.",
    "His elegy is one of the oldest Welsh poems, kept in the Black Book of Carmarthen and the Red Book of Hergest. The poet stands on the field of Llongborth, ‘the harbour of ships’, and sees the battle: the swift steeds under Geraint’s thighs, long-legged and fed on wheat, red as the eagle in their charge. ‘In Llongborth Geraint was slain,’ the poem says, ‘a brave man from the land of Dyfnaint.’ And it is in this poem that the poet says: ‘In Llongborth I saw Arthur, brave men who hewed with steel; emperor, ruler of the battle.’",
    "The Welsh romance Geraint son of Erbin, one of the three romances of Arthur’s court, tells how the young Geraint rode out from Caerleon after the knight who had insulted Gwenhwyfar’s maid, won the sparrow-hawk tournament for Enid daughter of Ynywl, and brought her back to be married at Arthur’s court, before going home to rule his father’s kingdom. Churches in Cornwall, among them Gerrans on the Roseland, keep his name."],
   ["Brenin Dyfnaint oedd Geraint fab Erbin, teyrnas Brythoniaid y de-orllewin, yn Nyfnaint a Chernyw, yn y dyddiau pan ddaliai’r Brythoniaid holl orllewin yr ynys o hyd. Mae’r achau Cymreig yn ei osod yn llinach frenhinol Dyfnaint, mab Erbin fab Custennin.",
    "Mae ei farwnad yn un o’r cerddi Cymraeg hynaf, a gedwir yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin a Llyfr Coch Hergest. Saif y bardd ar faes Llongborth ac yn gweld y frwydr: y meirch buain dan forddwyd Geraint, hirgoes, wedi’u porthi ar wenith, yn goch fel yr eryr yn eu rhuthr. ‘Yn Llongborth y lladdwyd Geraint,’ medd y gerdd, ‘gŵr dewr o wlad Dyfnaint.’ Ac yn y gerdd hon y dywed y bardd: ‘Yn Llongborth y gwelais i Arthur, gwŷr dewr a gymynent â dur; ymherodr, llywiawdr llafur.’",
    "Mae’r rhamant Gymraeg Geraint fab Erbin, un o dair rhamant llys Arthur, yn adrodd sut y marchogodd Geraint ifanc o Gaerllion ar ôl y marchog a sarhaodd forwyn Gwenhwyfar, ennill twrnamaint y gwalch i Enid ferch Ynywl, a dod â hi’n ôl i’w phriodi yn llys Arthur, cyn mynd adref i reoli teyrnas ei dad. Mae eglwysi yng Nghernyw, Gerrans ar y Roseland yn eu plith, yn cadw ei enw."]),
 P("enid", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Enid ferch Ynywl", "Enid ferch Ynywl", "The faithful heroine of Geraint and Enid", "Arwres ffyddlon Geraint ac Enid", "Geraint_and_Enid", "heroes",
   "The heroine of the Welsh romance Geraint son of Erbin. Her husband doubted her and made her ride ahead of him in silence, yet her warnings saved his life again and again.",
   "Arwres y rhamant Gymraeg Geraint fab Erbin. Amheuodd ei gŵr hi a’i gorfodi i farchogaeth o’i flaen heb ddweud gair, ond achubodd ei rhybuddion ei fywyd dro ar ôl tro.",
   ["Enid was the daughter of Earl Ynywl, who had lost his lands and lived in a ruined castle, and she served her father and mother in a faded dress with a gracious heart. When young Geraint came to the town seeking lodging, she cared for his horse and set food before him, and he asked for her hand. He fought for her at the tournament of the sparrow-hawk, overthrew the knight who had insulted Gwenhwyfar’s maid, and brought Enid to Arthur’s court at Caerleon, where Gwenhwyfar herself dressed her for the wedding.",
    "The heart of the romance is Enid’s faithfulness. On a long and dangerous journey Geraint ordered her to ride ahead of him and not to speak to him whatever she saw. Again and again she saw armed men lying in wait for him, and again and again she broke the order and warned him, choosing his life over his word. Each time he was ready, and won. In the end Geraint saw the truth of her heart, and they went home together to rule his father’s kingdom.",
    "The Triads count Enid with Dyfr Golden-Hair and Tegau Eurfron as one of the Three Splendid Maidens of Arthur’s court. In 1859 the English poet Tennyson retold her story in his Idylls of the King, and Enid became a much-loved name in Wales and across the English-speaking world."],
   ["Merch yr Iarll Ynywl oedd Enid. Collasai ei thad ei diroedd ac roedd yn byw mewn castell adfeiliedig, ac roedd hi’n gweini ar ei thad a’i mam mewn gwisg dreuliedig â chalon raslon. Pan ddaeth Geraint ifanc i’r dref yn chwilio am lety, gofalodd am ei farch a gosod bwyd o’i flaen, a gofynnodd yntau am ei llaw. Ymladdodd drosti yn nhwrnamaint y gwalch, gorchfygu’r marchog a sarhaodd forwyn Gwenhwyfar, a dod ag Enid i lys Arthur yng Nghaerllion, lle gwisgodd Gwenhwyfar ei hun hi ar gyfer y briodas.",
    "Calon y rhamant yw ffyddlondeb Enid. Ar daith hir a pheryglus gorchmynnodd Geraint iddi farchogaeth o’i flaen a pheidio â siarad ag ef beth bynnag a welai. Dro ar ôl tro gwelodd wŷr arfog yn llechu amdano, a dro ar ôl tro torrodd y gorchymyn a’i rybuddio, gan ddewis ei fywyd ef o flaen ei air. Bob tro roedd yn barod, ac yn ennill. Yn y diwedd gwelodd Geraint wirionedd ei chalon, ac aethant adref ynghyd i reoli teyrnas ei dad.",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn cyfrif Enid gyda Dyfr Wallt Euraid a Thegau Eurfron yn un o Dair Rhiain Ardderchog Llys Arthur. Yn 1859 adroddodd y bardd Seisnig Tennyson ei stori yn ei Idylls of the King, a daeth Enid yn enw annwyl yng Nghymru ac ar draws y byd Saesneg."]),
 P("gwyn-ap-nudd", "saints", "Age of Arthur", "Gwyn ap Nudd", "Gwyn ap Nudd", "King of Annwfn, the Otherworld", "Brenin Annwfn", "Gwyn_ap_Nudd", "heroes",
   "The king of Annwfn, the Welsh Otherworld, who rides in Arthur’s great hunt in Culhwch ac Olwen and speaks of the fallen heroes in the Black Book of Carmarthen. Arthur himself made peace between him and Gwythyr son of Greidawl.",
   "Brenin Annwfn, Arallfyd y Cymry, sy’n marchogaeth yn helfa fawr Arthur yn Culhwch ac Olwen ac yn sôn am yr arwyr a syrthiodd yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin. Arthur ei hun a wnaeth heddwch rhyngddo a Gwythyr fab Greidawl.",
   ["Gwyn son of Nudd is the king of Annwfn, the Otherworld of Welsh tradition, and the leader of its hunt. Culhwch ac Olwen says that God put in him the might of the spirits of Annwfn, lest the world be destroyed, and the giant Ysbaddaden says that Twrch Trwyth can never be hunted without him. So Gwyn rides in Arthur’s great hunt across the south of Wales.",
    "The same tale tells how Arthur made peace in the Otherworld. Gwyn and Gwythyr son of Greidawl both loved Creiddylad, daughter of Lludd Silver-Hand, the fairest maiden in the island, and their war shook the North. Arthur came and gave judgement: the maiden would stay in her father’s house, and Gwyn and Gwythyr would fight for her every first day of May until the day of judgement, and the one who won on that day would have her.",
    "In the Dialogue of Gwyddno Garanhir and Gwyn ap Nudd, in the Black Book of Carmarthen, Gwyn tells of the battles where the heroes of Britain fell, and says: ‘I have been where Llacheu was slain, son of Arthur.’ The Life of Saint Collen places his hall on the top of Glastonbury Tor. Dafydd ap Gwilym called the mist on the mountain ‘the land of Gwyn’s people’, and in Welsh folk tradition Cŵn Annwn, the hounds of Annwfn, are his."],
   ["Gwyn ap Nudd yw brenin Annwfn, Arallfyd traddodiad Cymru, ac arweinydd ei helfa. Medd Culhwch ac Olwen i Dduw roi ynddo nerth ysbrydion Annwfn, rhag difetha’r byd, a dywed y cawr Ysbaddaden na ellir byth hela’r Twrch Trwyth hebddo. Felly mae Gwyn yn marchogaeth yn helfa fawr Arthur ar draws de Cymru.",
    "Mae’r un chwedl yn adrodd sut y gwnaeth Arthur heddwch yn yr Arallfyd. Carai Gwyn a Gwythyr fab Greidawl ill dau Greiddylad, merch Lludd Llaw Ereint, y forwyn decaf yn yr ynys, ac ysgydwodd eu rhyfel y Gogledd. Daeth Arthur a rhoi barn: arhosai’r forwyn yn nhŷ ei thad, ac ymladdai Gwyn a Gwythyr amdani bob Calan Mai hyd ddydd brawd, a’r un a enillai’r dydd hwnnw a’i câi.",
    "Yn Ymddiddan Gwyddno Garanhir a Gwyn ap Nudd, yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin, mae Gwyn yn adrodd am y brwydrau lle syrthiodd arwyr Prydain, ac yn dweud: ‘Mi a fûm lle lladdwyd Llacheu, mab Arthur.’ Mae Buchedd Collen Sant yn gosod ei neuadd ar ben Tor Glastonbury. Galwodd Dafydd ap Gwilym y niwl ar y mynydd yn ‘dir tylwyth Gwyn’, ac yn nhraddodiad gwerin Cymru, ef biau Cŵn Annwn."]),

 # ---------- Seers and saints ----------
 P("myrddin", "saints", "fl. late 6th century", "Myrddin", "Myrddin", "Prophet and bard", "Proffwyd a bardd", "Myrddin_Wyllt", "words",
   "The prophet-bard of Welsh tradition, whose poems are in the Black Book of Carmarthen. Welsh tradition holds that Caerfyrddin, Carmarthen, carries his name, and Geoffrey of Monmouth made him Arthur’s counsellor, Merlin.",
   "Bardd-broffwyd traddodiad Cymru, y mae ei gerddi yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru mae Caerfyrddin yn dwyn ei enw, a gwnaeth Sieffre o Fynwy ef yn gynghorwr i Arthur, Merlin.",
   ["Myrddin is the prophet of Welsh tradition. The Welsh Annals record the battle of Arfderydd in 573, where Gwenddolau son of Ceidio, a king of the Old North, fell, and Welsh tradition holds that Myrddin was Gwenddolau’s bard. After the battle, the old poems say, he lived in the forest of Celyddon, the Caledonian Forest, with the creatures of the wild, and there the gift of prophecy came upon him.",
    "His poems are in the Black Book of Carmarthen. In Yr Afallennau, The Apple Trees, he speaks to a sweet apple tree that hides him from his enemies, and in Yr Oianau he speaks to a little pig, his only companion. Through both he foretells the day when the Britons will rise again under Cadwaladr and Cynan and win back the island. The same book keeps a dialogue between Myrddin and Taliesin, and the Red Book of Hergest a conversation between Myrddin and his sister Gwenddydd.",
    "In about 1135 Geoffrey of Monmouth published his Prophecies of Merlin, and then made Merlin a great figure of his History: the wise man who explains the dragons to Gwrtheyrn, raises the stones of the Giants’ Dance, and makes possible the birth of Arthur. Gerald of Wales wrote in 1188 that there were two Myrddins: Myrddin Emrys, born at Carmarthen, and Myrddin Wyllt of Celyddon. Welsh tradition holds that Caerfyrddin is Myrddin’s fort, that he sleeps in a cave on Bryn Myrddin, Merlin’s Hill, just outside the town, and that Carmarthen was guarded by Merlin’s Oak, whose last pieces are kept in the town today."],
   ["Myrddin yw proffwyd traddodiad Cymru. Mae Blwyddnodion Cymru’n cofnodi brwydr Arfderydd yn 573, lle syrthiodd Gwenddolau fab Ceidio, brenin yn yr Hen Ogledd, ac yn ôl traddodiad Cymru Myrddin oedd bardd Gwenddolau. Wedi’r frwydr, medd yr hen gerddi, bu fyw yng Nghoed Celyddon gyda chreaduriaid y gwyllt, ac yno daeth dawn proffwydoliaeth arno.",
    "Mae ei gerddi yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin. Yn Yr Afallennau mae’n siarad â phren afalau melys sy’n ei guddio rhag ei elynion, ac yn Yr Oianau mae’n siarad â pherchell bach, ei unig gydymaith. Drwy’r ddwy mae’n rhagweld y dydd pan gyfyd y Brythoniaid eto dan Gadwaladr a Chynan ac ennill yr ynys yn ôl. Mae’r un llyfr yn cadw ymddiddan rhwng Myrddin a Thaliesin, a Llyfr Coch Hergest sgwrs rhwng Myrddin a’i chwaer Gwenddydd.",
    "Tua 1135 cyhoeddodd Sieffre o Fynwy ei Broffwydoliaethau Myrddin, ac yna gwneud Myrddin yn ffigwr mawr yn ei Hanes: y gŵr doeth sy’n esbonio’r dreigiau i Wrtheyrn, yn codi meini Côr y Cewri, ac yn gwneud geni Arthur yn bosibl. Ysgrifennodd Gerallt Gymro yn 1188 fod dau Fyrddin: Myrddin Emrys, a aned yng Nghaerfyrddin, a Myrddin Wyllt o Gelyddon. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, caer Myrddin yw Caerfyrddin, mae’n cysgu mewn ogof ar Fryn Myrddin, ychydig y tu allan i’r dref, a gwarchodid Caerfyrddin gan Dderwen Myrddin, y cedwir ei darnau olaf yn y dref heddiw."]),
 P("dyfrig", "saints", "d. c. 550", "Saint Dyfrig", "Dyfrig Sant", "The bishop who crowned Arthur", "Yr esgob a goronodd Arthur", "Dubricius", "faith",
   "Born in Ergyng, founder of monasteries at Henllan and Mochros and bishop of the south-east. Welsh tradition holds that he crowned Arthur at Caerleon. He died on Ynys Enlli.",
   "Fe’i ganwyd yn Ergyng, sefydlodd fynachlogydd yn Henllan a Mochros, a bu’n esgob y de-ddwyrain. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, ef a goronodd Arthur yng Nghaerllion. Bu farw ar Ynys Enlli.",
   ["Dyfrig was born in Ergyng, the Welsh kingdom between the Wye and the Monnow, at Madley on the Wye. His mother was Efrddyl, daughter of Peibio, king of Ergyng. He founded a great monastery at Henllan, Hentland, where, his Life says, he taught two thousand scholars for seven years, and another at Mochros, Moccas, the moor of pigs, which he chose because a white sow and her piglets showed him the place.",
    "He is the oldest of the saints of the south-east. The Life of Saint Samson, written in the seventh century, tells how Bishop Dyfrig ordained the young Samson, and the Book of Llandaff makes him the first bishop of Llandaff and the teacher of Teilo. The churches that bear his name, Llanddyfrig and St Devereux among them, lie along the Wye and in Gwent and Ergyng.",
    "Welsh tradition holds that it was Dyfrig who crowned Arthur. In Geoffrey of Monmouth’s History he is archbishop of the City of the Legions, Caerleon. He sets the crown on Arthur’s head, and before the battle of Badon he stands on a hill and blesses the army of the Britons. In old age he left his see and went to live as a hermit on Ynys Enlli, the island of twenty thousand saints, and he died there. In 1120 his remains were carried to Llandaff, where he is honoured in the cathedral today. His feast day is 14 November."],
   ["Ganed Dyfrig yn Ergyng, y deyrnas Gymreig rhwng Gwy a Mynwy, ym Madle ar lan Gwy. Ei fam oedd Efrddyl, merch Peibio, brenin Ergyng. Sefydlodd fynachlog fawr yn Henllan, lle, medd ei Fuchedd, y dysgodd ddwy fil o ysgolheigion am saith mlynedd, ac un arall ym Mochros, rhos y moch, a ddewisodd am fod hwch wen a’i pherchyll wedi dangos y lle iddo.",
    "Ef yw’r hynaf o saint y de-ddwyrain. Mae Buchedd Samson Sant, a ysgrifennwyd yn y seithfed ganrif, yn adrodd sut yr ordeiniodd yr Esgob Dyfrig y Samson ifanc, ac mae Llyfr Llandaf yn ei wneud yn esgob cyntaf Llandaf ac yn athro Teilo. Mae’r eglwysi sy’n dwyn ei enw, Llanddyfrig a St Devereux yn eu plith, ar hyd Gwy ac yng Ngwent ac Ergyng.",
    "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, Dyfrig a goronodd Arthur. Yn Hanes Sieffre o Fynwy ef yw archesgob Dinas y Llengoedd, Caerllion. Mae’n gosod y goron ar ben Arthur, a chyn brwydr Baddon mae’n sefyll ar fryn ac yn bendithio byddin y Brythoniaid. Yn ei henaint gadawodd ei esgobaeth ac aeth i fyw’n feudwy ar Ynys Enlli, ynys yr ugain mil o saint, a bu farw yno. Yn 1120 cludwyd ei weddillion i Landaf, lle’i hanrhydeddir yn yr eglwys gadeiriol heddiw. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 14 Tachwedd."]),
 P("gwynllyw", "saints", "fl. c. 500", "Gwynllyw", "Gwynllyw", "King of Gwynllŵg and saint", "Brenin Gwynllŵg a sant", "Gwynllyw", "rulers",
   "King of Gwynllŵg, the land between the Usk and the Rhymney, who won Gwladys daughter of Brychan with Arthur’s help in the Life of Saint Cadog. Their son was Cadog. He ended his days as a hermit where Newport Cathedral stands.",
   "Brenin Gwynllŵg, y tir rhwng Wysg a Rhymni, a enillodd Wladys ferch Brychan gyda help Arthur ym Muchedd Cadog Sant. Eu mab oedd Cadog. Diweddodd ei ddyddiau’n feudwy lle saif Eglwys Gadeiriol Casnewydd.",
   ["Gwynllyw was a son of Glywys, the king who gave his name to Glywysing, and he ruled Gwynllŵg, the coastal land between the Usk and the Rhymney that still carries his name in English as Wentloog. He was a great warrior in his youth, and the Welsh remembered him as Gwynllyw Filwr, Gwynllyw the Soldier.",
    "He loved Gwladys, daughter of Brychan, king of Brycheiniog, and when her father would not give her to him, he came with three hundred men and carried her away. Brychan rode after them with his army. The Life of Saint Cadog, written at Llancarfan, tells what happened next. On the hill between the two kingdoms sat Arthur, with Cai and Bedwyr, playing at dice. Arthur saw the two armies, and heard that Gwynllyw was in his own country, and he rode down with his companions to stand by Gwynllyw. Brychan’s men drew back, and Gwynllyw brought his bride home.",
    "Their first son was Cadog, one of the great saints of Wales, founder of Llancarfan. Under Cadog’s teaching Gwynllyw and Gwladys turned in later life to God. Gwynllyw built a church on the hill above the Usk, and lived there as a hermit until he died. The church grew into St Woolos, the cathedral of Newport, whose English name is his own worn smooth by time. His feast day is 29 March."],
   ["Mab i Glywys oedd Gwynllyw, y brenin a roddodd ei enw i Glywysing, a rheolai Wynllŵg, y tir ar yr arfordir rhwng Wysg a Rhymni sy’n dal i ddwyn ei enw yn Saesneg fel Wentloog. Roedd yn rhyfelwr mawr yn ei ieuenctid, a chofiai’r Cymry ef fel Gwynllyw Filwr.",
    "Carai Wladys, merch Brychan, brenin Brycheiniog, a phan na roddai ei thad hi iddo, daeth gyda thri chant o wŷr a’i chipio ymaith. Marchogodd Brychan ar eu hôl â’i fyddin. Mae Buchedd Cadog Sant, a ysgrifennwyd yn Llancarfan, yn adrodd beth ddigwyddodd wedyn. Ar y bryn rhwng y ddwy deyrnas eisteddai Arthur, gyda Chai a Bedwyr, yn chwarae disiau. Gwelodd Arthur y ddwy fyddin, a chlywed bod Gwynllyw yn ei wlad ei hun, a marchogodd i lawr gyda’i gymdeithion i sefyll gyda Gwynllyw. Ciliodd gwŷr Brychan, a dygodd Gwynllyw ei briodferch adref.",
    "Eu mab cyntaf oedd Cadog, un o saint mawr Cymru, sylfaenydd Llancarfan. Dan ddysgeidiaeth Cadog troes Gwynllyw a Gwladys at Dduw yn ddiweddarach yn eu bywyd. Cododd Gwynllyw eglwys ar y bryn uwchben Wysg, a bu fyw yno’n feudwy hyd ei farw. Tyfodd yr eglwys yn St Woolos, eglwys gadeiriol Casnewydd, y mae ei henw Saesneg yn enw iddo ef wedi’i dreulio’n llyfn gan amser. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 29 Mawrth."]),
 P("gwladys", "saints", "fl. c. 500", "Saint Gwladys", "Santes Gwladys", "Daughter of Brychan, mother of Cadog", "Merch Brychan, mam Cadog", "Gwladys", "faith",
   "Daughter of Brychan Brycheiniog, wife of King Gwynllyw and mother of Saint Cadog. Arthur himself stood by her marriage in the Life of Cadog. In later life she lived as a holy woman, and the ruins of her chapel stand above Gelligaer.",
   "Merch Brychan Brycheiniog, gwraig y Brenin Gwynllyw a mam Cadog Sant. Arthur ei hun a safodd o blaid ei phriodas ym Muchedd Cadog. Yn ddiweddarach yn ei bywyd bu fyw fel gwraig sanctaidd, a saif adfeilion ei chapel uwchben Gelligaer.",
   ["Gwladys was one of the daughters of Brychan, king of Brycheiniog, the father of saints. Gwynllyw, king of Gwynllŵg, loved her and carried her away from her father’s court, and when Brychan’s army followed, Arthur, Cai and Bedwyr came down from the hill to stand by the young king, as the Life of Saint Cadog tells. So Gwladys came to Gwynllŵg as its queen.",
    "Her first son was Cadog, founder of the great monastery of Llancarfan and one of the most loved saints of Wales. Through Cadog’s teaching she and Gwynllyw gave their later years to God. The Life tells how she lived at first near her husband by the Usk, at Pencarn, and would bathe in the cold river at night in prayer, winter and summer.",
    "Later she went up to the moorland above the Rhymney valley, and the ruins of Capel Gwladys still stand on Gelligaer Common, with a carved stone found there kept nearby. Churches at Bargoed and elsewhere bear her name. Her feast day is 29 March, the same as her husband’s."],
   ["Un o ferched Brychan, brenin Brycheiniog, tad y saint, oedd Gwladys. Carai Gwynllyw, brenin Gwynllŵg, hi a’i chipio o lys ei thad, a phan ddilynodd byddin Brychan, daeth Arthur, Cai a Bedwyr i lawr o’r bryn i sefyll gyda’r brenin ifanc, fel yr adrodd Buchedd Cadog Sant. Felly y daeth Gwladys i Wynllŵg yn frenhines.",
    "Ei mab cyntaf oedd Cadog, sylfaenydd mynachlog fawr Llancarfan ac un o saint mwyaf annwyl Cymru. Drwy ddysgeidiaeth Cadog rhoddodd hi a Gwynllyw eu blynyddoedd olaf i Dduw. Mae’r Fuchedd yn adrodd sut y bu fyw ar y dechrau ger ei gŵr ar lan Wysg, ym Mhencarn, ac yr ymdrochai yn yr afon oer gyda’r nos mewn gweddi, gaeaf a haf.",
    "Yn ddiweddarach aeth i fyny i’r rhos uwchben Cwm Rhymni, ac mae adfeilion Capel Gwladys yn sefyll o hyd ar Gomin Gelligaer, a maen cerfiedig a gafwyd yno wedi’i gadw gerllaw. Mae eglwysi ym Margod ac mewn mannau eraill yn dwyn ei henw. Ei dydd gŵyl yw 29 Mawrth, yr un ag un ei gŵr."]),
 P("carannog", "saints", "fl. c. 530", "Saint Carannog", "Sant Carannog", "The saint whom Arthur gave land", "Y sant y rhoddodd Arthur dir iddo", "Carantoc", "faith",
   "A prince of Ceredigion who became a hermit at Llangrannog. In his Life, he tamed a great serpent for Arthur on the shore of the Severn Sea, and Arthur gave him land in return.",
   "Tywysog o Geredigion a ddaeth yn feudwy yn Llangrannog. Yn ei Fuchedd, dofodd sarff fawr i Arthur ar lan Môr Hafren, a rhoddodd Arthur dir iddo’n dâl.",
   ["Carannog was of the royal line of Ceredigion: his Life makes him a grandson of Ceredig son of Cunedda, the king who gave his name to the land. He turned from the crown to the life of a hermit, and lived in a cave above the little cove that now bears his name, Llangrannog, where his statue looks out over the beach.",
    "The Life of Carannog tells that he set his altar on the waves of the Severn Sea and followed wherever God carried it. It came to shore in the land ruled by Cadwy and Arthur, at Dindraethwy, Dunster, and Arthur was there, searching for a great serpent that was laying waste the country of Carrum. Carannog went to the serpent, laid his stole about its neck and led it, gentle as a lamb, to the fortress, and told the people not to kill it. In return Arthur gave him back his altar and gave him the land at Carrum, Carhampton, to build a church.",
    "He carried the faith on to Cornwall, where Crantock bears his name, and to Brittany, where Carantec does too. In Wales he is remembered at Llangrannog in Ceredigion. His feast day is 16 May."],
   ["Roedd Carannog o linach frenhinol Ceredigion: mae ei Fuchedd yn ei wneud yn ŵyr i Geredig fab Cunedda, y brenin a roddodd ei enw i’r wlad. Troes oddi wrth y goron at fywyd meudwy, a bu fyw mewn ogof uwchben y gilfach fach sy’n dwyn ei enw heddiw, Llangrannog, lle mae ei gerflun yn edrych dros y traeth.",
    "Mae Buchedd Carannog yn adrodd iddo osod ei allor ar donnau Môr Hafren a’i dilyn lle bynnag y’i dygai Duw. Daeth i’r lan yn y wlad a reolid gan Gadwy ac Arthur, yn Nindraethwy, Dunster, ac roedd Arthur yno, yn chwilio am sarff fawr a oedd yn difrodi gwlad Carrum. Aeth Carannog at y sarff, gosod ei ystola am ei gwddf a’i harwain, mor dyner ag oen, at y gaer, a dweud wrth y bobl am beidio â’i lladd. Yn dâl rhoddodd Arthur ei allor yn ôl iddo, a rhoi iddo’r tir yn Carrum, Carhampton, i godi eglwys.",
    "Cariodd y ffydd ymlaen i Gernyw, lle mae Crantock yn dwyn ei enw, ac i Lydaw, lle mae Carantec hefyd. Yng Nghymru fe’i cofir yn Llangrannog yng Ngheredigion. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 16 Mai."]),
]

